export default "# The Solis DIY concept and the market\n\n## What this site is\n- \"Solis DIY\" is a concept by Rok Skrinjar: a guide, an AI adviser (Skobi) and an order planner that shows how Solis Timber could sell its timber directly to hobby woodworkers in Slovenia. It is not affiliated with Solis Timber. No orders are sent; the inquiry button only shows what the customer and the sawmill would receive.\n- Pages: the guide (/solis-diy/index.html: projects, Wood 101, by-products, the company), the planner (planner.html: 13 project templates or an own cut list, nesting into 3 m / 4 m stock, price, delivery, inquiry preview) and the business case (business.html).\n- The planner: choose a project, set sizes, choose the wood variant, or load a cut list from the chat. It groups parts by product and section, nests them into stock lengths (3 or 4 m, about 20 mm overmeasure, 4 mm kerf), counts cuts (0.30 € each), computes m³, weight, an illustrative price and the DIY-store comparison. Delivery: pickup in Straža (free), parcel for pieces up to 2 m (5.99–29.99 € by weight), van up to 4 m and 1 t (25 € up to 20 km, then 0.70 €/km), truck for long beams (60 € up to 20 km, then 1.20 €/km). Orders under 100 € have an 8 € small-order fee.\n\n## Market facts (October 2026)\n- Slovenian sawmills' median price for kiln-dried joinery boards: 415 €/m³ net (Slovenian Forestry Institute, Feb 2026). Construction timber C24: 285–300 €/m³ net.\n- Slovenian DIY stores sell planed spruce boards at about 1,800–2,300 €/m³ incl. VAT (OBI 19 mm boards 2.89–6.29 €/m in 2 m lengths; Slovenijales planed battens cut to length 2,090–2,330 €/m³). KVH 100×100 / 120×120 at OBI about 1,460–1,500 €/m³, special order with about 3 weeks' delivery. Glulam beams 1,060–1,830 €/m³. Pellets 6.89–7.49 € per 15 kg bag (national average 456 €/t, Aug 2026).\n- So hobbyists pay about 3.5–4 times the sawmill price for dry planed timber, mostly in short (2 m), thin sections. No Slovenian sawmill was found that sells to hobbyists online with a cut-to-size configurator; Slovenijales (a merchant) cuts planed battens to 300–2,000 mm for pickup only.\n- Abroad: sawmills and merchants such as Holz-Shop.com (AT), baustoffshop.de, Zuschnittprofi (DE), Holz Gar (Bavarian sawmill with web shop) and Scottish Wood (UK sawmill) sell direct with configurators or cut lists.\n- Center Rog in Ljubljana runs a public woodworking lab (membership 15 €, monthly 60 €); a natural partner for courses and pickups.\n\n## Concept prices (illustrative, incl. 22 % VAT, ex works Straža)\n- Planed spruce/fir 1,190 €/m³ (larch to order ×1.45). Quarter-sawn 1,450 €/m³ (knot-free +20 %). Dry sawn 640 €/m³. Fresh sawn 390 €/m³ (+25 €/m³ HT). Glulam BSH GL24h 1,050 €/m³. KVH (coming) 890 €/m³.\n- These are about 35–45 % below DIY-store prices and still 2–3 times the wholesale value. They are estimates for the concept, not Solis Timber prices.\n- Examples from the planner with default sizes: raised bed 200×100×60 cm in planed spruce about 130 € of timber; workbench 160×60 cm about 200 €; picnic table 180 cm about 230 €; pergola 3×3 m about 560 €.\n\n---\n\n# CATALOG (the only products, species and sections the planner accepts)\n\nUse these ids in cutlist blocks. Sections are thickness x width in mm (planed sizes for planed/radial, nominal for sawn). Board products (planed, radial, dry, fresh) come in 3 m and 4 m stock and are cut to length; parts max 4000 mm. bsh and kvh are cut to length from long beams.\n\n- species `sf`: spruce/fir / smreka/jelka, price factor 1, density 470 kg/m³\n- species `pine`: pine / bor, price factor 1, density 520 kg/m³\n- species `larch`: larch (to order) / macesen (po naročilu), price factor 1.45, density 600 kg/m³\n\n## product `planed`: Planed timber / Skobljan les\n- Smooth, square boards and battens, ready to use. Uses: Indoor furniture, shelves, garden furniture, cladding, kids' items (rounded edges).\n- species: `sf`, `larch`; moisture KD 8–12 %\n- sections: 20x45, 20x95, 20x145, 28x95, 28x145, 28x195, 45x45, 45x70, 45x95, 45x145, 45x195, 70x70, 95x95\n- part length 150–4000 mm; stock lengths 3000, 4000 mm\n- concept price 1190 €/m³ incl. VAT (Slovenian DIY-store average about 1900 €/m³)\n- option: sharp or rounded edges\n\n## product `radial`: Quarter-sawn timber / Radialen les\n- Radially sawn, no pith: boards stay flat. Uses: Tabletops, doors, frames, sauna benches, cladding, beehive frames.\n- species: `sf`; moisture KD 8–12 %\n- sections: 20x45, 20x95, 28x95, 28x145, 42x95\n- part length 150–4000 mm; stock lengths 3000, 4000 mm\n- concept price 1450 €/m³ incl. VAT; knot-free +20 %\n\n## product `dry`: Dry sawn timber / Suh žagan les\n- Kiln dried, surface straight from the saw. Uses: Frames, garden builds, rustic looks, stock you will plane yourself.\n- species: `sf`, `pine`, `larch`; moisture KD 12–20 %\n- sections: 24x100, 24x150, 30x50, 50x100, 50x150, 80x80, 100x100\n- part length 300–4000 mm; stock lengths 3000, 4000 mm\n- concept price 640 €/m³ incl. VAT\n\n## product `fresh`: Fresh sawn timber / Svež žagan les\n- The cheapest, but wet: it will shrink and move. Uses: Compost bins, rough garden builds, formwork, pallets, stock to air-dry yourself.\n- species: `sf`, `pine`; moisture svež / fresh > 30 %\n- sections: 24x100, 24x150, 50x100, 80x80, 100x100\n- part length 300–4000 mm; stock lengths 3000, 4000 mm\n- concept price 390 €/m³ incl. VAT (Slovenian DIY-store average about 430 €/m³); HT heat treatment +25 €/m³\n\n## product `bsh`: Glulam BSH GL24h / Lepljen nosilec BSH GL24h\n- Straight, stable, strong, up to 13.5 m. Cut to length. Uses: Posts and beams for pergolas, carports, lintels; under a roof.\n- species: `sf`; moisture KD 10–12 %\n- sections: 80x80, 100x100, 120x120, 80x160, 100x200, 120x200, 120x240\n- part length 1000–13500 mm; cut to length\n- concept price 1050 €/m³ incl. VAT (Slovenian DIY-store average about 1300 €/m³)\n\n## product `kvh`: Structural timber KVH / Konstrukcijski les KVH (COMING SOON, can be requested)\n- New: finger-jointed C24, planed. Coming soon. Uses: Shed frames, rafters, studs, visible indoor beams.\n- species: `sf`; moisture 15 ± 3 %\n- sections: 60x120, 60x160, 80x120, 100x100, 120x120\n- part length 1000–13000 mm; cut to length\n- concept price 890 €/m³ incl. VAT (Slovenian DIY-store average about 1450 €/m³)\n\n## Extras (by-products)\n- A1 pellets, 15 kg bag / Peleti A1, vreča 15 kg: 6.49 €, from 66 bags 5.99 €\n- Offcut kindling, 25 l bag / Pričeljki za podžig, vreča 25 l: 4.5 €\n- Dry sawdust for bedding, 50 l bag / Suha žagovina za steljo, vreča 50 l: 3.9 €\n- Ground bark mulch, 1 m³ / Mleto lubje za zastirko, 1 m³: 34 €\n\nFees: cutting 0.3 € per cut; orders under 100 € pay 8 €.\n\n---\n\n# PLANNER TEMPLATES\n\nLink a template as a markdown link, e.g. [Raised bed](planner.html#p=raised-bed). Parameters are in mm and can be set in the link, e.g. planner.html#p=raised-bed&L=3000&W=1200&H=600&v=larch.\n\n## `raised-bed`: Raised garden bed / Visoka greda (difficulty 1, outdoor)\n- Boards stacked in rows around upright corner posts. Spruce in soil contact lasts a few years, larch much longer. Longer beds need middle posts so the long sides do not bow out.\n- params: L=Length 1000–4000 (default 2000), W=Width 600–1500 (default 1000), H=Height 300–900 (default 600)\n- variants: planed (Planed spruce (neat, 3–5 years)), dry (Dry sawn (cheapest)), larch (Larch (lasts longest))\n- tools: Circular or hand saw, cordless driver, square, stainless screws 5×60. Finish: Nothing inside (soil contact). Oil or glaze outside if you like.\n- mistakes: Boards thinner than 25 mm bow out. Posts dug into the soil rot first. Wood dipped in preservative is not for vegetables.\n\n## `planter`: Planter box / Cvetlično korito (difficulty 1, outdoor)\n- The raised bed's little sibling, with a slatted base and feet so it never stands in water.\n- params: L=Length 400–1500 (default 800), W=Width 300–600 (default 400), H=Height 300–600 (default 400)\n- variants: planed (Planed spruce), larch (Larch)\n- tools: Hand or mitre saw, driver, sandpaper. Finish: Oil or glaze outside, nothing inside; line it with perforated foil.\n- mistakes: No gaps in the base, so water pools. A box standing flat on the floor rots from below.\n\n## `compost`: Compost bin / Kompostnik (difficulty 1, outdoor)\n- Slats with 2 cm air gaps and a removable front so turning the compost is easy. Two bays: one matures while you fill the other.\n- params: W=Bay width 800–1500 (default 1000), D=Depth 800–1500 (default 1000), H=Height 600–1200 (default 900), n=Bays 1–3 (default 2)\n- variants: fresh (Fresh sawn (cheapest)), dry (Dry sawn)\n- tools: Hand or circular saw, driver, tape measure. Finish: None.\n- mistakes: No gaps, so the compost cannot breathe. A front that does not come off. Posts dug into the soil.\n\n## `firewood`: Firewood store / Drvarnica (difficulty 2, outdoor)\n- A mono-pitch roof, open sides and a raised floor. Firewood needs air from every side, so the walls stay open. Roofing (corrugated sheets or felt) is not in the cut list.\n- params: L=Length 1500–3600 (default 2000), D=Depth 600–1200 (default 1000), H=Front height 1400–2000 (default 1800)\n- variants: dry (Dry sawn spruce), larch (Larch posts)\n- tools: Circular saw, driver, level, post bases, string line. Finish: Pigmented glaze on visible parts.\n- mistakes: Closed walls: the wood cannot breathe. Logs straight on the ground. A roof without an overhang. Post feet in splash water: keep 30 cm clear.\n\n## `pergola`: Pergola / Pergola (difficulty 3, outdoor)\n- Posts and beams in glulam (BSH), which does not twist or check like solid squared timber. The sections here are typical sizes for estimating quantities, not a structural calculation. If you add a roof (polycarbonate) it becomes a canopy: it needs a snow-load calculation and a permit check.\n- params: L=Length (beams) 2500–4000 (default 3000), W=Depth (rafters) 2500–3400 (default 3000), H=Post height 2200–2800 (default 2500)\n- variants: sf (Glulam + planed spruce rafters), larch (Glulam + larch rafters)\n- tools: Circular saw, drill, long level, timber screws, H anchors, a helper. Finish: Pigmented glaze or paint; seal beam ends, slope or cap the top faces.\n- mistakes: A roof without a structural calculation. Posts concreted into the ground rot at the collar. No knee braces.\n\n## `bench`: Garden bench / Vrtna klop (difficulty 1, outdoor)\n- A seat of three thick boards with gaps, a frame of 45×95 battens. Seat height 45 cm.\n- params: L=Length 1000–2000 (default 1200)\n- variants: sf (Planed spruce, rounded edges), larch (Larch)\n- tools: Circular or mitre saw, driver, optional pocket-hole jig. Finish: Exterior oil or glaze, end grain sealed.\n- mistakes: No gaps between boards, so water pools. Legs standing in wet grass: add stones or glides.\n\n## `picnic`: Picnic table with benches / Piknik miza s klopema (difficulty 2, outdoor)\n- The classic A-frame table. Cut the leg angles on a mitre saw and pull every joint tight with carriage bolts.\n- params: L=Length 1500–2400 (default 1800)\n- variants: sf (Planed spruce), larch (Larch (heavier, lasts longer))\n- tools: Mitre saw, drill, spanner, M10 carriage bolts, clamps. Finish: Exterior oil or glaze, end grain sealed.\n- mistakes: No gaps between boards. Bolts not re-tightened once the wood dries. A spruce table weighs about 90 kg: it takes two to move.\n\n## `workbench`: Workbench / Delovna miza (difficulty 2, indoor)\n- A top of 45×95 battens glued on edge (after Paul Sellers' popular bench). Doubled legs and a lower shelf make it rigid. A heavy bench is a good bench.\n- params: L=Length 1200–2400 (default 1600), W=Depth 500–800 (default 600), H=Height 800–1000 (default 900), shelf=Lower shelf options [0, 1] (default 1)\n- variants: sf (Planed spruce 45×95)\n- tools: At least 6–8 clamps, PVA glue, circular saw, driver, hand plane or belt sander. Finish: A thin oil or nothing.\n- mistakes: Gluing damp or uneven battens. A top clamped up twisted. No rails or shelf, so it racks.\n\n## `wall-shelf`: Wall shelves / Stenske police (difficulty 1, indoor)\n- A shelf from one wide board or two glued on edge. Wide flat-sawn boards like to cup; quarter-sawn ones stay flat.\n- params: L=Shelf length 400–1500 (default 800), D=Depth 150–290 (default 190), n=Number of shelves 1–8 (default 3)\n- variants: planed (Planed spruce), radial (Quarter-sawn (stays flat))\n- tools: Drill, level, plugs for brick, concrete or plasterboard. Finish: Oil, hard-wax oil or water-based lacquer.\n- mistakes: Brackets too far apart: for books 70–80 cm at most. Wrong wall plugs. A wide flat-sawn board cups.\n\n## `bookshelf`: Bookshelf / Knjižna omara (difficulty 2, indoor)\n- Sides and shelves from two 145 mm boards glued on edge (290 mm). Shelves sit in dados or on pins. Always anchor it to the wall.\n- params: H=Height 1000–2200 (default 1800), W=Width 500–1000 (default 800), D=Depth 140–290 (default 290), n=Shelves (incl. top and bottom) 3–8 (default 5), back=Slatted back options [0, 1] (default 1)\n- variants: planed (Planed spruce 28×145), radial (Quarter-sawn 28×145)\n- tools: Clamps, router or shelf-pin jig, driver, sander. Finish: Oil or water-based lacquer.\n- mistakes: Not anchored to the wall (tip-over risk). All growth rings facing the same way. Shelves too long for heavy books.\n\n## `bed`: Bed frame / Posteljni okvir (difficulty 2, indoor)\n- Rails 45×195, legs 95×95, slats on ledger strips. Above 140 cm wide add a centre beam with two legs.\n- params: MW=Mattress width options [900, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800] (default 1600), ML=Mattress length options [1900, 2000, 2100] (default 2000), LH=Leg height 150–400 (default 300)\n- variants: sf (Planed spruce)\n- tools: Circular saw, driver, bed-rail brackets, sander. Finish: Oil or lacquer.\n- mistakes: Slat gaps too wide (often 6–7 cm at most). No centre support on a 160 cm bed. Squeaks: put felt under the slats.\n\n## `sauna`: Sauna benches / Klopi za savno (difficulty 2, indoor)\n- Seats from knot-free quarter-sawn boards on 45×70 frames. Fir has no resin canals, so it suits saunas. No screw may show on the seat: in the heat they burn.\n- params: L=Bench length 1000–3000 (default 2000), n=Tiers 1–3 (default 2)\n- variants: radial (Knot-free quarter-sawn spruce/fir)\n- tools: Mitre saw, round-over router bit, driver, stainless screws. Finish: None, or paraffin sauna oil. No lacquer.\n- mistakes: Visible screws on the seat. Knots and resin in the seat. No gaps, so the bench cannot dry.\n\n## `slat-wall`: Slat wall / Stena z letvicami (difficulty 1, indoor)\n- Vertical 20×45 slats with even gaps on horizontal battens. For real acoustic absorption add felt panels behind the slats.\n- params: W=Wall width 1000–5000 (default 3000), H=Height 2000–3000 (default 2600), G=Gap between slats 10–40 (default 20)\n- variants: radial (Quarter-sawn slats (straighter)), planed (Planed slats)\n- tools: Mitre saw, brad nailer or adhesive and screws, laser or level. Finish: Water-based lacquer or white oil (spruce yellows in light).\n- mistakes: Wood not acclimatised: gaps open in winter. Battens not flush. Electrics forgotten.\n\nOther ideas shown on the guide page (answer them yourself, with a cutlist block): Carport / Nadstrešek za avto; Adirondack chair / Stol Adirondack; Slatted privacy screen / Zasebna ograja iz letvic; Small deck / Lesena terasa; Dog house / Pasja uta; Chicken coop / Kokošnjak; AŽ beehive frames / Satniki za AŽ panj; Nest box / Ptičja hišica; Tool shed / Vrtna uta za orodje; Greenhouse / Rastlinjak; Outdoor kitchen / Zunanja kuhinja; Loft stair / Strme stopnice na podstrešje; Floating shelf / Lebdeča polica; Low platform bed / Nizka postelja na podestu; Picture frames / Okvirji za slike; Acoustic slat panel / Akustični panel; Cat tree / Mačje drevo; Kids' table and stools / Otroška mizica s stolčkoma; Pallet-style sofa / Kavč v slogu palet; Entryway coat rack / Obešalnik za predsobo; Serving board / Servirna deska\n\n---\n\n# Solis Timber d.o.o.: company facts\n\nPublic facts for the Skobi adviser. Sources: solistimber.com (read 2026-10-28), AJPES via companywall.si, Sloexport, FSC certificate database, Delo (12 Jul 2018), EY Family Business Book Slovenia 2022, Si21 (Apr 2021), 24ur (Mar 2023), Kmečki glas (Feb 2025).\n\n## Who they are\n- Full name: Solis Timber, predelava lesa, d.o.o. (until 27 Jul 2021: Solis Straža d.o.o.). Address: Na žago 6, 8351 Straža, Slovenia (south-east Slovenia, 9 km from Novo mesto, 8 km from the Kočevski Rog forests). Railway station Straža nearby.\n- An innovative family sawmill and wood-processing company. Main activity: sawmilling of softwoods (spruce, fir, pine; larch logs are also bought). Products: boards, beams, planks, battens, mostly for construction, glued panels, packaging, window and door lamellas, and planed timber. By-products: offcuts, chips, sawdust and pellets.\n- Founded in 1990 by Jože Zoran as \"Žagarstvo Jože Zoran s.p.\", started in his garage. Management today: founder and director Jože Zoran, co-founder and procurist Betka Zoran, director Kaja Zoran Ledinek (MSc wood technology, Biel; earlier sales at Binderholz; led sales from 2017).\n- Timeline (company site): 1999 first employee and sawing hall; 2001 first computer-controlled band saw; 2003 office building; 2007 Solis Straža d.o.o. founded; 2009 new production plant and product range; 2010 finished-goods warehouse; 2015 production moved to the former Novoles site in Straža; 2017 Kaja joined, staff grew from 7 to 20 in two years; 2018 investment in digitalisation, new sawing line, debarking with scanning and a boiler house (about €5 M); 2021 renamed Solis Timber; Dec 2022 bought 3.5 ha in the industrial zone for expansion; 2024 automatic planing line; 2025 new computer-controlled EWD band saw.\n- EU/Slovenian co-financing \"Strong&Solid\" (decision 16 Feb 2023, up to €3,435,960.90): extension of the plant, a planing-and-sorting line, a finger-jointing line and dust extraction, to introduce KVH (finger-jointed structural timber).\n- Size: more than 60,000 m³ of softwood logs a year (2025), which puts it in the top size class of Slovenian sawmills (only about 2 % of ~510 Slovenian sawmills saw more than 60,000 m³). About 30–40 employees. Revenue €17.1 M in 2025 (AJPES), about 87 % from exports.\n- Markets: more than 30 countries in Europe, the Middle and Far East, Africa and America, mostly without middlemen (e.g. Saudi Arabia, UAE, Japan, Austria, Sweden, Türkiye, India). The company site says \"33 years on the market, 234 satisfied customers, 33 countries\".\n- Today Solis sells B2B/wholesale. It has supplied planed timber to DIY stores abroad, but has no shop, price list or online sales for private customers in Slovenia. (This is the gap the Solis DIY concept explores.)\n\n## Quality and certificates\n- Logs from Slovenia, Austria, Italy, Croatia, Czechia and South-East Europe; sawn on German EWD technology. Log purchasing is all year round, logs measured electronically with Microtec 3D scanning; diameters 10–120 cm; standard log length 4 m.\n- Kiln drying in controlled chambers, \"perfect and even drying\"; attention to grain orientation and appearance.\n- FSC Chain of Custody FSC-C124693 (BV-COC-124693, valid to 30 Mar 2030): all products FSC 100 %.\n- CE: visually strength-graded structural timber to EN 14081-1 (factory production control certificate).\n- FITO certificate for ISPM-15 (heat treatment of packaging wood, HT).\n- \"Production 100 % without waste\"; the company says it stores more than 60,000 t of CO₂ a year.\n- Motto on the site: \"Strong. Solid. Solis.\"\n\n## Products (as published by Solis)\n- Dry sawn timber (suh žagan les): spruce/fir/pine; centre or side cut; boxed-heart-free possible; custom dimensions; kiln dried KD 8–20 %; lengths 3–4 m; graded to DIN. Uses: glulam, CLT and KVH production, construction, packaging, furniture.\n- Planed timber (skobljan les): spruce/fir; sharp or rounded edges; profiled timber possible; centre or side cut; custom dimensions; KD 8–20 %; 3–4 m. Uses: BSH/CLT/KVH production, DIY products, garden furniture, floor and wall cladding.\n- Radial timber (radialen les): spruce/fir; side cut + radial; with or without knots; no pith; widths 50–150 mm; KD 8–20 %; 3–4 m. Uses: windows and doors, furniture, garden furniture, saunas, wall cladding, parquet. Shrinks and warps less, lasts longer.\n- Fresh sawn timber (svež žagan les): spruce/fir/pine; optional bio-protective dip; IPPC (HT) and CE possible; 3–4 m. Uses: wooden packaging, support in concrete construction, simple structures.\n- Glulam BSH: spruce, visible and industrial quality, GL24, widths 80–240 mm, heights 120 mm+, lengths up to 13.5 m, KD 10–12 %. Uses: houses, halls, carports, lintels, beams and posts.\n- Biomass: sawdust (fresh or dry; pellets, animal bedding), chips G30 without bark, offcuts 7–40 cm (firewood), ground bark (fuel, fertiliser), A1 spruce/fir pellets in 15 kg bags.\n- Coming: KVH finger-jointed structural timber.\n\n## Contact (public)\n- Phone +386 7 30 84 450, info@solistimber.com, Mon–Fri 07:00–16:00. Only mention this if the user asks how to reach the real company, and say the concept itself takes no orders.\n\n---\n\n# DIY woodworking knowledge base: softwoods from a Slovenian sawmill\n\nKnowledge base for a beginner DIY advisor (guide, chatbot, order configurator) built on Solis Timber's product range: dry sawn, planed, radial (quarter-sawn), fresh sawn, glulam BSH GL24h, upcoming KVH, plus by-products.\n\nResearched 2026-10-28. Source tags such as **[S5]** point to the source list at the end.\n\n**How to read the figures**\n- A number with a source tag is quoted from that source.\n- **Rule of thumb** marks practitioner guidance or my own estimate. It is not a measured or standardised value.\n- **Arithmetic** blocks show how a quantity was calculated, so it can be checked and reused in a configurator.\n- Where sources disagree, both values are given.\n\n**Gaps to know about**\n- I could not reach primary Holzforschung Austria (HFA) publications. The species data come from the equivalent German institute sheets: LWF Bayern / Holzforschung München, based on DIN 68364 and Grosser & Zimmer [S5–S8]. Moisture and coating data come from the USDA Wood Handbook [S11] and Swedish Wood [S9, S10].\n- The EN standards themselves (EN 350, EN 335, EN 338, EN 14080, EN 1912) are paywalled. Their values are taken from manufacturer, institute or trade summaries, and every value carries its source.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Solis product to beginner use: quick map\n\n| Solis product (SL name) | What the beginner gets | Best for | Watch out |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dry sawn timber** (suh žagan les), spruce/fir/pine, KD 8–20 %, centre or side cut, boxed-heart-free possible, DIN graded, custom sizes, up to 4 m | Rough, saw-textured surface. Kiln dried, so dimensionally \"settled\". | Structural frames, garden builds, rustic looks, anything you will plane yourself. Rough-sawn surfaces hold outdoor stains and paints longer than planed ones [S11]. | Rough surface gives splinters; dimensions vary by ±1–2 mm. To get smooth, square stock you need a planer/thicknesser, or you can order planed. |\n| **Planed timber** (skobljan les), spruce/fir, sharp or rounded edges, profiles possible, KD 8–20 % | Ready-to-use, smooth, square boards and battens. | Indoor furniture, shelves, garden furniture, cladding (profiled), kids' items (rounded edges). | The planed size is smaller than the sawn size (see 2.2). Order the moisture content to suit the use (see 1.6). |\n| **Radial / quarter-sawn** (radialen les), spruce/fir, with or without knots, no pith, widths 50–150 mm, KD 8–20 % | Growth rings run near-vertical through the thickness, so the board moves about half as much across its width and hardly cups (see 1.5). | Tabletop glue-ups, frames, doors, window-like joinery, sauna benches and lining, interior cladding, parquet, beehive frames, picture frames. | Narrow (max 150 mm) and costs more. Use it where stability matters, not for rough frames. |\n| **Fresh sawn** (svež žagan les), spruce/fir/pine, optional bio-protective dip, optional HT (ISPM-15) | Cheap, wet (well above 20 % MC). It will shrink, check and possibly warp as it dries. | Compost bins, rough sheds, formwork, pallets, temporary or \"rustic\" outdoor builds; air-drying stock for later projects. | Do not build tight-fitting furniture or anything indoors from it. Treat dipped wood as chemically treated (see 4.6). |\n| **Glulam BSH GL24h**, spruce, visible or industrial quality, up to 13.5 m, KD 10–12 % | Straight, stable, strong beams and posts in long lengths. | Carports, pergolas, lintels, long beams, posts, loft beams. | Needs a roof over it outdoors (service class 1–2). Structural sizes must be calculated (see 2.5). Long beams are heavy. |\n| **KVH** (upcoming): finger-jointed structural solid timber | C24, 15 ± 3 % MC, planed, pith cut or pith-free [S14] | Framing for sheds, studs, rafters, visible interior beams. | KVH is for indoor or covered use (service classes 1–2). |\n| **By-products**: sawdust, chips G30, offcuts 7–40 cm, ground bark, A1 pellets 15 kg | See section 4. | Heating, bedding, mulch, compost, kindling, crafts. | Heating pellets are not smoker pellets (4.1). |\n\n---\n\n## 1. The four species for hobbyists\n\n### 1.1 Key numbers\n\n| Property | Spruce (Norway, *Picea abies*), SL: smreka | Silver fir (*Abies alba*), SL: jelka | Scots pine (*Pinus sylvestris*), SL: rdeči bor | European larch (*Larix decidua*), SL: macesen |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mean density at 12–15 % MC, DIN 68364 [S5] | 460 kg/m³ (range 330–680) | 460 kg/m³ ([S5]); 470 ([S6]) | 520 kg/m³ (range 350–750) | 600 kg/m³ |\n| Average dried weight (Wood Database, 12 %) | 405 kg/m³ [S1] | 415 kg/m³ [S2] | 550 kg/m³ [S3] | 575 kg/m³ [S4] |\n| Janka hardness [S1–S4] | 1,680 N (380 lbf) | 1,420 N (320 lbf) | 2,420 N (540 lbf) | 3,290 N (740 lbf) |\n| Brinell hardness, N/mm², along / across grain, DIN 68364 [S5] | 32 / 12 | 30 / 16 | 40 / 19 | 53 / 19 |\n| Bending strength, small clear specimens [S5] | 80 N/mm² | 80 | 85 | 99 |\n| Modulus of elasticity [S5] | 11,000 N/mm² | 11,000 | 11,000 | 13,800 |\n| Shrinkage green to oven-dry, radial / tangential / volume [S5] | 3.6 / 7.8 / 11.9–12.0 % | 3.8 / 7.6 / 11.5–11.7 % | 4.0 / 7.7 / 12.1–12.4 % | 3.3 / 7.8 / 11.4–11.8 % |\n| Movement per 1 % MC change (5–20 % MC), radial / tangential [S5] | 0.19 / 0.39 % | 0.12–0.16 / 0.28–0.35 % | 0.19 / 0.36 % | 0.14–0.18 / 0.28–0.36 % |\n| EN 350 durability class, heartwood, ground contact (1 = very durable, 5 = not durable) | **4** [S5, S9] | **4** [S6] | **3–4** [S8, S9] | **3–4** [S7, S9] |\n| Sapwood / heartwood | No colour difference | No colour difference | Wide pale sapwood (2–10 cm, usually 3–4 cm), red-brown heartwood [S8] | Very narrow sapwood (often under 2 cm), red-brown heartwood [S7] |\n| Resin | Resin canals and frequent resin pockets [S5] | **No resin canals, no resin pockets** [S6] | Many large resin canals [S8] | Resin canals, high resin content [S7] |\n| Smell | Resinous when fresh [S5] | No resin smell. Wetwood (bacterial wet heart) smells sour when fresh; the smell goes as it dries [S6]. | Mild, resinous [S3] | Strong aromatic, resinous; persists even when dry [S7] |\n\nThe Brinell values above are DIN 68364 values cited by LWF. Other teaching tables give slightly different across-grain values for spruce and fir, so treat about 12–16 N/mm² for both as the realistic band.\n\n**What this means for a beginner**\n- Spruce and fir are soft. You can dent them with a fingernail or a dropped tool, and they are easy to cut, plane and nail.\n- Larch is about twice as hard as spruce (Janka 3,290 vs 1,680 N), and pine sits between them.\n- For comparison, hard maple, the classic cutting-board wood, is about 6,450 N (1,450 lbf) on the Wood Database. That is roughly four times spruce.\n\n### 1.2 Workability, glue and finishes\n\n**Spruce**\n- Saws, planes, routes, drills and sands easily by hand or machine.\n- Easy to nail and screw. Glues without problems with PVA (thermoplastic) and thermoset glues [S5].\n- Problem features: knots, resin pockets (Harzgallen), spiral grain and compression wood. Compression wood (reaction wood) is the main cause of strong distortion [S5].\n- Takes paint, glaze, oil, wax and lacquer well. Cut-open resin pockets can disturb film formation and need pre-treatment [S5].\n- Staining views differ:\n  - LWF calls spruce excellent to stain, with the stain bringing out the earlywood/latewood contrast [S5].\n  - The Wood Database reports blotchy results with pigment and dye stains and suggests a sanding sealer, gel stain or toner [S1].\n  - Advice for the bot: **always test the stain on an offcut first.**\n- Low extractive content (2.3 %). Iron does not corrode in contact with it but gives a faint grey stain in damp wood. Copper and brass do not stain it [S5].\n\n**Fir**\n- Works like spruce. It is a little more brittle and \"woolly\" and splinters more easily, but works without real difficulty [S6].\n- Glues well, including wetwood [S6].\n- Being resin-free, it is \"an excellent substrate for coatings\" [S6].\n- After drying it is stable and \"moves less than spruce\" [S6].\n- Preferred where spruce resin would be a nuisance [S6]:\n  - sauna interior lining\n  - containers exposed to acids and alkalis\n  - baking boards, because it is taste- and odour-neutral\n- Usually traded together with spruce as \"Fichte/Tanne\" (Fi/Ta) [S5, S6].\n\n**Pine**\n- Easy to work. Resin gums up tools [S8].\n- Glues well, except very resinous pieces [S8].\n- Takes any finish. Very resinous boards should be sorted out, and you should de-resin before staining or when the piece will be heated [S8].\n- Moist sapwood is very prone to **blue stain**. It is cosmetic only and does not reduce strength [S8].\n- Extractives about 9 %. Iron gives a faint grey stain when damp [S8].\n\n**Larch**\n- Works well but resin can overheat saw blades and make them wander [S7].\n- **Pre-drill for heavy nails and screws** to avoid splitting [S7].\n- Glues fine. Takes lacquer, glaze, matt finishes and wax well, but **stains poorly** [S7].\n- Resin pockets and dark knots can disturb film finishes [S7].\n- Slightly acidic (pH 4.7). In damp conditions iron reacts with its tannins and gives grey to blue-black stains, so **use stainless fasteners outdoors** [S7].\n- Sanding can leave dips because soft earlywood and hard latewood wear at different rates [S4].\n\n### 1.3 Durability: indoor vs outdoor\n\nEN 350 rates the heartwood's natural resistance to decay fungi, mainly in ground contact. The classes are 1 very durable, 2 durable, 3 moderately durable, 4 slightly durable and 5 not durable [S9].\n\n**Spruce**\n- Class 4, and \"rather quickly perishable\" in ground contact [S5].\n- Above ground it does better than the class suggests. It takes up liquid water only slowly, so with good detailing it reaches decay-level moisture (above 20 %) only slowly [S5, S9].\n- That is why spruce is widely used for façade cladding in Austria and Bavaria [S5].\n\n**Fir**\n- Class 4 [S6].\n- Considered better than spruce where wood is permanently or alternately wet [S6].\n\n**Pine heartwood**\n- Class 3–4 in ground contact, but \"good\" resistance when weathered above ground [S8].\n- **Sapwood is highly susceptible** to fungi and insects [S8].\n\n**Larch heartwood**\n- Normatively class 3–4, but more weather-resistant than all other native softwoods [S7].\n- Under DIN 68800-1 its heartwood may be used in use classes 2 and 3.1 without preventive chemical treatment [S7].\n- Larch sapwood is class 5 [S7].\n\n**Lifetime of heartwood stakes (20×50×500 mm) in ground contact, Swedish data [S9]:** spruce under 5 years, pine and larch 5–10 years.\n\n**Use classes (EN 335 / DIN 68800, German GK)**\n\n| Use class | Situation | Beginner example | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UC1 / GK1 | Indoors, dry | Shelf, bed, bookshelf | Any species, no protection needed [S19] |\n| UC2 / GK2 | Covered, occasional condensation or wetting | Carport beams under the roof, firewood shed frame | Spruce/fir fine if it can dry out |\n| UC3.1 / GK3.1 | Outdoors above ground, water runs off and dries quickly | Garden bench, cladding, pergola with good detailing | Spruce/fir with good detailing and a coating; larch or Douglas heartwood if in doubt [S20] |\n| UC3.2 / GK3.2 | Outdoors above ground, water stays (horizontal surfaces, joints) | Decking, the top of a horizontal beam | Load-bearing decking is usually GK3.2, or GK4 if dirt collects [S20]. Spruce here is short-lived. |\n| UC4 / GK4 | Ground or freshwater contact | Raised bed walls, fence posts in soil | Spruce under 5 years [S9]. Avoid soil contact by design, or accept the part as a consumable. |\n\nThe German \"dry\" threshold is a mean wood moisture **below 20 %**. Keeping wood below 20 % is the main protection against decay fungi, and using kiln-dried wood is the simplest protection against insects [S12, S20].\n\n### 1.4 Typical defects and what they mean\n\n| Defect | Seen in | Meaning for the DIYer |\n|---|---|---|\n| Knots (sound, loose, black \"pin knots\") | All. Larch has many small black \"nail knots\" [S7]. | Loose knots fall out. Knots near an edge weaken beams. Knots bleed resin and extractives through paint (see 5.1). |\n| Resin pockets / pitch (Harzgallen) | Spruce, pine, larch, not fir [S5, S6, S11] | Resin oozes when warm, including through paint. Sun-exposed and sauna parts are worst [S11]. |\n| Compression wood (Druckholz, red/hard streaks) | Spruce, pine | Strong warping and lengthwise shrinkage. Graders sort it out for dimension-stable parts [S5]. |\n| Spiral grain (Drehwuchs) | Spruce | Twists as it dries [S5]. |\n| Pith / boxed heart in the piece | Centre-cut pieces | More splitting and checking. Choose boxed-heart-free (herzfrei) or pith-cut (herzgetrennt); see 2.1. |\n| Blue stain (Bläue) | Pine sapwood mainly; also spruce, fir, larch | Cosmetic only. Unlimited blue stain is allowed in structural grades [S8]. A sign the wood was wet. |\n| Wetwood (Nasskern) | Fir | Very wet core (up to 220 % MC fresh), sour smell. Dries out; the odour disappears [S6]. |\n| Shrinkage checks (cracks) | Thick sections, especially with pith | Normal in solid and glulam timber. For glulam, cracks up to 1/6 of the width (no tension across the grain) are not structurally critical [S17]. |\n\n### 1.5 Quarter-sawn (radial) vs flat-sawn movement\n\nWood shrinks and swells about **twice as much tangentially (along the growth rings) as radially** (across them):\n- spruce: 0.39 vs 0.19 % per 1 % MC change [S5]\n- spruce tangential/radial ratio: 2.1 [S1, S5]\n\nA flat-sawn board (rings lying flat) therefore changes width a lot and **cups**. A quarter-sawn or radial board (rings near-vertical) changes width about half as much and stays flat [S11].\n\n**Formula (USDA Wood Handbook) [S11]:** ΔD = D × C × (MC_end − MC_start)\n- D is the dimension.\n- C is the movement coefficient per 1 % MC. Use the tangential coefficient for flat-sawn width and the radial coefficient for quarter-sawn width.\n- The formula is valid between about 6 and 14 % MC.\n\n**Arithmetic: 145 mm wide spruce board drying from 15 % to 8 % MC (7 points)**\n\n| Source of coefficient | Flat-sawn (tangential) | Quarter-sawn (radial) |\n|---|---|---|\n| LWF/DIN, Norway spruce, 0.39 / 0.19 % [S5] | 145 × 0.0039 × 7 = **4.0 mm** | 145 × 0.0019 × 7 = **1.9 mm** |\n| Wood Handbook, North American red/Sitka spruce, 0.274 / 0.130 % [S11] | 145 × 0.00274 × 7 = **2.8 mm** | 145 × 0.0013 × 7 = **1.3 mm** |\n\nUsing both sources, expect a 145 mm spruce board to shrink by roughly **3–4 mm flat-sawn or 1.3–2 mm quarter-sawn** when it goes from \"outdoor dry\" to \"heated-room dry\".\n\n**Arithmetic: 800 mm tabletop indoors, seasonal swing 8 % → 12 % (4 points)**\n- All flat-sawn: 800 × 0.0039 × 4 ≈ **12.5 mm** of seasonal movement.\n- Mixed grain, using the average coefficient (0.39 + 0.19) / 2 = 0.29 %: 800 × 0.0029 × 4 ≈ **9.3 mm**.\n- Therefore never glue or screw a solid top rigidly to a frame across its width. Use slotted holes, figure-8 fasteners or buttons.\n\n### 1.6 Moisture content: why kiln-dried matters and what to order\n\n**Basic physics**\n- Fresh softwood heartwood typically holds 30–50 % MC. Fir wetwood can reach 220 % [S6].\n- Wood only shrinks below the **fibre saturation point (about 30 % for softwoods)** [S12].\n- It then moves towards the **equilibrium moisture content (EMC)** set by the air's relative humidity (RH). RH is the main driver; temperature matters little [S10].\n- Install wood at the moisture content it will have in service, to avoid shrinkage cracks, gaps and distortion [S12].\n\n**Measured and recommended values**\n\n| Situation | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Heated homes, mid Sweden | Average 7.5 % over the year; 7–12 % in summer, **2–6 % in winter** (indoor RH 10–25 %) | [S10] |\n| Interior woodwork, furniture, flooring (US most areas) | Average **8 %**, individual pieces 6–10 % | [S11] |\n| Softwood for furniture, cabinets, millwork | Dried to 7–9 % | [S11] |\n| Interior components, German practice | **8–12 %** | [S13] |\n| Floorboards in heated rooms (Swedish delivery target) | 8 % | [S10] |\n| Exposed cladding and mouldings in heated rooms | 12 % | [S10] |\n| Exterior wood (US most areas) | Average **12 %**, pieces 9–14 % | [S11] |\n| Exterior components, German practice | **12–15 %** | [S13] |\n| Solid wood and glulam for exterior cladding (Swedish target) | 16 % | [S10] |\n| Outdoor EMC in a damp November (Malmö) | about 20 % | [S10] |\n| KVH structural timber | 15 ± 3 % | [S14] |\n| Maximum for painting or surface treatment | 16 % | [S10] |\n| Risk of paint blistering | above 20 % | [S11] |\n| Firewood (Slovenian small-combustion rules) | under 20 % | [S29] |\n\n**Practical ordering rule for Solis KD 8–20 %** (rule of thumb derived from the table above)\n\n| Use | Order MC |\n|---|---|\n| Indoor furniture, shelves, picture frames, tabletops | **8–10 %** |\n| Indoor cladding and structural parts in heated rooms | **10–12 %** |\n| Covered outdoor structures (carport, pergola, shed frame) and outdoor furniture | **12–16 %** |\n| Rough outdoor work (compost bin, firewood store) | **16–20 %**; fresh sawn is acceptable if you accept shrinkage |\n\n**Acclimatisation** (rule of thumb)\n- Bring the wood into the room where it will be used. Stack it flat on spacer sticks (\"stickers\") so air reaches all faces.\n- Wait **1–2 weeks** for furniture stock, longer for thick pieces or wet wood. Check with a moisture meter rather than counting days.\n- Resistance meters with hammer electrodes are accurate to about **±2 % MC** and better for batch averages than for single pieces [S10].\n- Measure about 300 mm from the end [S10].\n- Right after kiln drying to 16 %, the surface can be below 10 % while the core is still above 20 %. A rest period lets this gradient equalise [S10].\n\n---\n\n## 2. Sawn vs planed vs glulam vs KVH\n\n### 2.1 Terms the customer will meet\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Rough sawn** (sägerau, žagan) | The surface straight from the saw. It is not flat, square or smooth to furniture tolerance. Fine for frames, sheds and garden structures. For furniture you must surface-plane one face, joint one edge, then thickness-plane, or buy planed stock. |\n| **Planed / dressed** (gehobelt, skobljan) | Four sides planed, flat and square, smooth enough to finish after light sanding. |\n| **Centre cut** | Pieces from the middle of the log that contain the pith. More likely to check. |\n| **Side cut** | Pieces from outside the pith. Better quality and fewer checks. |\n| **Pith-cut** (herzgetrennt) | The pith was cut through, so the piece does not contain a whole boxed heart [S14]. |\n| **Boxed-heart-free** (herzfrei, brez stržena) | No pith at all. A separate heart plank of at least 40 mm is cut out [S14]. Least checking. |\n| **Radial / quarter-sawn** | Rings near-vertical to the face. Stable (see 1.5). |\n| **KD** | Kiln dried. Technically dried timber in a controlled kiln, at ≥ 55 °C for at least 48 h, to ≤ 20 % MC [S14]. |\n| **Sawn vs planed size** | Planing removes material. **Rule of thumb:** expect about 3–5 mm less per dimension. For example, 50×100 sawn becomes about 45×95 planed, and 24 mm sawn boards become about 19–21 mm planed. Ask Solis for its actual planed sizes. |\n\n### 2.2 Standard section sizes (Central European trade practice)\n\nNominal sizes for sawn timber in trade standards usually refer to **20 % MC** (Finnish standard) [S43]. EN 1313-1 covers permitted deviations and preferred sizes for softwood sawn timber.\n\nCommon sizes (thickness × width, mm). These are a rule-of-thumb list of what is commonly stocked in AT/SI/DE; Solis cuts to order.\n\n| Group | Typical sizes | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Battens / laths (letve) | 24×48, 30×50, 40×60 | Cladding battens, roof battens, small frames |\n| Boards (deske), sawn | 24 × 100–200 (24 mm is the classic \"1-inch\" board, colarica), 28 × 95–195 | Cladding, raised beds, shelving, sheathing |\n| Boards, planed / Nordic | 21×95, 21×145, 28×95, 28×145, 28×195 | Furniture, decking-like boards, cladding |\n| Thick planks (plohi) | 45/48/50 × 145–245 | Benches, tables, stair stringers |\n| Studs / frame timber | 45×70, 45×95, 45×145, 45×195, 60×120, 60×140 | Workbenches, sheds, rafters, joists |\n| Squared timber (morali, tramovi) | 60×60, 80×80, 100×100, 120×120, 140×140, 80×160, 100×200 | Posts, pergolas, carport columns |\n\n**KVH preferred cross-sections** (KVH-NSi, spruce/fir, C24; widths 60, 80, 100, 120 and 140 mm, heights 100–240 mm in 20 mm steps, not every combination) [S14]:\n- Typical stocked sizes: 60×120 to 60×240, 80×120 to 80×240, 100×100, 120×120, 140×140. Check the exact grid in table 4.1 of [S14] or with the supplier.\n- Maximum KVH section is about **140×240 mm**, limited by technical drying [S14]. Above 140 mm width, Duo/Trio or glulam is recommended [S14].\n\n**Lengths**\n- Finnish standard sawn lengths: 2.7–5.4 m in 300 mm steps [S43].\n- KVH and Duo/Trio: usually 13 m [S14].\n- Solis dry sawn: up to 4 m. Solis glulam: up to 13.5 m.\n\n### 2.3 Strength grading: S10 and C24 explained\n\n- **DIN 4074-1** grades softwood visually into S7, S10 and S13 by knots, ring width, slope of grain, cracks, wane and so on. **EN 338** defines strength classes C16, C24 and C30. **EN 1912** maps one to the other [S5, S16].\n- For spruce/fir: **S7 ≈ C16, S10 ≈ C24, S13 ≈ C30** [S16].\n- For spruce, S13 allows ring widths up to 4 mm and S10 up to 6 mm [S5].\n- In Austria, timber in load-bearing structures must be graded to ÖNORM DIN 4074-1 by law [S16].\n- \"TS\" means graded dry, at ≤ 20 % MC, which is required for building use in Germany [S14].\n\n| Strength class (EN 338) [S16] | C16 (S7) | C24 (S10) | C30 (S13) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Characteristic bending strength | 16 N/mm² | 24 N/mm² | 30 N/mm² |\n| Mean modulus of elasticity | 8,000 N/mm² | 11,000 N/mm² | 12,000 N/mm² |\n| Characteristic density | 310 kg/m³ | 350 kg/m³ | 380 kg/m³ |\n\n**Beginner meaning**\n- For furniture, grading barely matters; appearance (knots, straightness) matters more.\n- For anything that carries people, snow or a roof (loft ladder, pergola with a roof, carport, shed rafters), use **at least S10/C24** and have the sizes checked.\n\n### 2.4 KVH vs BSH vs Duo/Trio\n\n| Product | What it is | Moisture | Typical range | When to choose it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sawn / planed solid** (Solis) | Single piece of sawn timber | KD to order | Up to 4 m (Solis) | Furniture, small structures, cheapest per m³ |\n| **KVH** (Konstruktionsvollholz) | Graded C24 solid timber; planed or calibrated; usually finger-jointed into long lengths; \"Si\" visible grade or \"NSi\" non-visible grade [S14, S15] | **15 ± 3 %** [S14] | Up to about 140×240, usually 13 m [S14] | Framing, rafters, studs, visible beams indoors; dimensionally reliable. Service classes 1–2. |\n| **Duo/Trio beams** (Balkenschichtholz) | 2–3 (up to 9) boards or squared pieces glued face to face [S14] | Dry, stored conditioned [S14] | Duo ≤ 160×280, Trio ≤ 240×280 (EN 14080) [S14] | Bigger, more stable sections than KVH, with fewer cracks. Service classes 1–2; avoid extreme climate cycling [S14]. |\n| **Glulam BSH GL24h** (Brettschichtholz) | Many thin finger-jointed lamellas glued together (EN 14080) | Solis: KD 10–12 % | Up to 13.5 m (Solis); sections up to very large heights | Long spans, carports, lintels, posts. Surface qualities: industrial, visible (the default if nothing is agreed), selection [S17]. |\n\n**GL24h characteristic values (EN 14080, via secondary table) [S18]:**\n- bending strength 24 N/mm²\n- mean modulus of elasticity 11,500 N/mm²\n- characteristic density 385 kg/m³ (mean 420 kg/m³)\n\n### 2.5 When should a beginner choose which?\n\n1. **Indoor furniture and shelves:** planed spruce/fir at 8–10 % MC. Use radial stock for tabletops, doors and anything that must stay flat. Choose rounded edges for kids' items.\n2. **Garden furniture:** planed spruce/fir with a good coating, or larch (order larch specially; Solis buys larch logs). Choose rounded edges.\n3. **Raised beds, compost bins, firewood stores:** dry sawn (rough is fine) or fresh sawn for the cheapest option. Larch lasts longer.\n4. **Pergola or carport posts and beams:** glulam GL24h or KVH. These are straighter and crack less than solid squared timber.\n   - Spans and sections must come from a structural calculation for the local snow and wind load.\n   - Keep the glulam under a roof. It is made for service classes 1–2.\n   - In service class 3, durable species such as larch or Douglas heartwood are recommended [S17].\n5. **\"I don't own a planer\":** buy planed. A rough-sawn board needs a jointer and a thicknesser (or a lot of hand-planing) to become furniture stock.\n\n**Arithmetic: how heavy is a long glulam beam?** 100×200 mm × 13.5 m = 0.27 m³. At about 470 kg/m³ that is about **127 kg**. Plan for two or three people or a lifting aid.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DIY projects (33 ideas)\n\n### 3.0 Conventions and how to calculate quantities\n\n**Difficulty**\n- 1 = beginner: straight cuts and screws\n- 2 = intermediate: joinery, glue-ups, angles, small structures\n- 3 = advanced: structural or large builds, possibly needing a permit or an engineer\n\n**Method used throughout**\n1. Make a cut list: how many parts, of which section and length.\n2. Group parts by section.\n3. **Nest the parts into stock lengths** (2.0 / 2.5 / 3.0 / 4.0 m). This matters more than total metres, because offcuts are wasted.\n4. Add **10–15 % waste** (rule of thumb: defects, saw kerf about 3 mm per cut, mistakes).\n5. Optionally convert to m³ for pricing: m³ = n × t × w × L (all in m).\n6. Mass ≈ m³ × density. Spruce is about 460 kg/m³ at 12–15 % MC and larch about 600 [S5, S7].\n\n**Useful shortcuts**\n- **Metres of board per m² of surface** = 1 / (board width + gap), in m.\n  - Example: 24×95 boards with no gap need 1 / 0.095 = 10.5 m/m².\n  - Slats 45 mm wide with 20 mm gaps need 1 / 0.065 = 15.4 m/m².\n- **Number of slats** = surface width / (slat width + gap), rounded up.\n- Imperial plans (US YouTube and Pinterest):\n  - 2x4 = 38×89 mm. The nearest EU size is 45×95 or 45×70.\n  - 1x6 = 19×140 mm. The nearest is 20/21×145 planed.\n  - 8 ft = 2.44 m.\n  - Re-check any dimension that depends on board thickness.\n\n**Generic tool kits**\n- **Basic:** tape measure, pencil, square, hand saw or circular saw, cordless drill/driver, clamps, sandpaper or orbital sander, safety glasses, hearing protection, dust mask.\n- **Intermediate:** add a mitre saw, jigsaw, pocket-hole jig or dowel jig, router, more clamps.\n- **Advanced:** add a table saw, planer/thicknesser, levels and string lines, post anchors, ladder or scaffold.\n\n**Assumed sizes for the large builds.** For pergola, carport, shed, greenhouse, stairs and decking, the sections given are **typical kit-style sizes for budgeting only**. Real sizes must come from a structural calculation for the site's snow zone (Slovenia ranges from lowland to alpine snow loads) and from the applicable building rules (see Q&A 17).\n\n### 3.1 Garden and outdoor\n\n**1. Raised garden bed, 200 × 100 × ~60 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** dry sawn or planed boards 28×145. Larch for a longer life; spruce is a consumable in soil contact (under 5 years for stakes in soil [S9]; untreated pine/fir beds about 3–5 years, a hobby-source estimate [S31]).\n- **Corner and middle posts:** 45×70 (or 45×45 at the corners).\n\nArithmetic:\n- Rows: 4 × 145 mm = 580 mm, about 60 cm.\n- Per row: 2 long sides of 2.00 m, and 2 short sides of 1.00 − 2 × 0.028 = 0.944 m (they fit between the long sides).\n- Long sides: 8 pieces × 2.0 m = **8 boards**.\n- Short sides: 8 pieces × 0.944 m, at 2 per 2.0 m board = **4 boards**.\n- Boards: **12 × 28×145×2000**, which is 12 × 0.028 × 0.145 × 2.0 = **0.097 m³**.\n- Posts: 4 corners and 2 mid-posts on the long sides (stops the 2 m sides bowing outwards) = 6 × 0.60 m = 3.6 m of 45×70, so **2 × 45×70×2000**.\n- Screws: (16 board pieces × 2 ends + 8 mid-post crossings) × 2 screws = about **80 exterior screws** (5×60 mm, stainless for larch).\n- Soil to fill it: 1.94 × 0.94 × 0.58 ≈ **1.06 m³**.\n\n- **Tools:** basic kit.\n- **Finish:** none inside (soil contact). Optionally line the inside with a dimpled membrane, open at the top so the boards can dry; this is a rule of thumb. Raw linseed oil on the inside is suggested by hobby sources [S31]. The RHS notes lining helps keep treated-wood chemicals out of the soil [S31].\n- **Beginner mistakes:**\n  - boards thinner than about 25 mm, which bow\n  - no mid brace on 2 m sides\n  - posts dug into the soil\n  - using wood with a \"bio-protective dip\" for vegetables (ask what it is)\n  - buying too little soil\n\n**2. Planter box, 80 × 40 × 40 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor or balcony.**\n- **Product:** planed 24×95 (spruce or larch); corner posts 45×45.\n\nArithmetic:\n- 4 rows × 95 mm = 380 mm.\n- Per row: 2 × 0.80 m and 2 × (0.40 − 0.048) = 0.352 m.\n- 8 long pieces of 0.80 m at 2 per 2 m board = 4 boards.\n- 8 short pieces plus 8 base slats of 0.352 m (inner length 0.752 / 0.095 ≈ 8) = 16 pieces at 5 per 2 m board = 4 boards.\n- Total: **8 × 24×95×2000**.\n- Posts: 4 × 0.38 m = 1.52 m, so **1 × 45×45×2000**.\n\n- **Tools:** basic kit.\n- **Finish:** exterior oil or glaze outside; none inside.\n- **Mistakes:** no drainage holes or gaps in the base; base resting on the floor (put it on feet).\n\n**3. Compost bin, single bay 100 × 100 × 90 cm, slatted**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** fresh sawn or dry sawn 24×95 (ideal cheap use of fresh sawn); posts 80×80.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Slat pitch with 2 cm air gaps: 95 + 20 = 115 mm. Height 900 / 115 = 7.8, so 8 slats per side.\n- Slats: 4 sides × 8 = 32 slats × 1.0 m (cut to 0.99 m to allow for kerf) = 32 m, so **8 × 24×95×4000** (4 slats per board).\n- Volume: 32 × 0.024 × 0.095 = **0.073 m³**.\n- Posts: **4 × 80×80×1000**, standing on stones or post bases.\n\n- **Tools:** basic kit.\n- **Finish:** none.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - no air gaps\n  - front not removable: use slats sliding in a slot between two battens\n  - posts dug in, which makes them rot first\n\n**4. Firewood shed / log store (drvarnica), 200 × 100 cm footprint, mono-pitch roof**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** dry sawn spruce. Larch for the posts if possible.\n- Typical parts (rule-of-thumb sizes):\n  - Posts 100×100: 3 front × 1.8 m + 3 back × 1.6 m = 10.2 m.\n  - Top plates 60×120: 2 × 2.2 m.\n  - Rafters 45×95: 4 × 1.6 m = 6.4 m (spacing about 65 cm).\n  - Floor joists 60×120: 3 × 2.0 m on stones.\n  - Floor slats 28×145 with gaps: 2.0 / (0.145 + 0.02) ≈ 12 × 1.0 m = 12 m.\n  - Roof: battens 30×50 every 50 cm (4 × 2.4 = 9.6 m) under corrugated sheets, or roof boards 24×145 (1.55 / 0.145 ≈ 11 × 2.4 m = 26.4 m) under roofing felt.\n- **Capacity:** about 2.0 × 1.0 × 1.6 m ≈ 3.2 m³ of stacked wood.\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit, post bases.\n- **Finish:** pigmented glaze or paint on exposed parts.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - closed walls (logs need airflow)\n  - wood stacked directly on the ground\n  - roof without an overhang\n  - post feet sitting in splash water (keep about 30 cm clear [S19])\n\n**5. Pergola, 3 × 3 m, freestanding, about 2.4 m clear height**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** glulam GL24h or KD sawn spruce/larch. Typical sections (rule of thumb, verify):\n  - Posts 120×120: 4 × 2.5 m\n  - Beams 80×200: 2 × 3.6 m (30 cm overhang each side)\n  - Rafters 45×145 at 50 cm spacing: 3.0 / 0.5 + 1 = 7 × 3.6 m\n  - Knee braces 45×95: 4 × 0.8 m\n\nArithmetic (volume):\n- Posts: 4 × 2.5 × 0.12 × 0.12 = 0.144 m³.\n- Beams: 2 × 3.6 × 0.08 × 0.20 = 0.115 m³.\n- Rafters: 7 × 3.6 × 0.045 × 0.145 = 0.164 m³.\n- Total about **0.42 m³** plus braces.\n\n- **Tools:** advanced kit, H-post anchors with concrete footings.\n- **Finish:** pigmented glaze or paint. Cap the beam ends; slope rafter tops or protect them with a cover board and drip edge [S19].\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - adding a roof (polycarbonate) without recalculating for snow; a roofed pergola is a canopy and a different building category\n  - posts in concrete, which makes them rot at the collar\n  - missing bracing, so it racks\n  - untreated horizontal tops\n\n**6. Carport, single, about 3 × 5 m**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Covered outdoor** (service class 2).\n- **Product:** **glulam GL24h** (straight, stable, long lengths). Kit-style layout for budgeting only:\n  - 6 posts 120×120 × 2.6 m (3 per side, about 2.4 m apart)\n  - 2 beams 120×200 × 5.6 m\n  - 9 rafters 80×160 × 3.6 m (5.6 / 0.75 + 1 ≈ 9)\n\nArithmetic:\n- Posts: 6 × 2.6 × 0.0144 = 0.225 m³.\n- Beams: 2 × 5.6 × 0.024 = 0.269 m³.\n- Rafters: 9 × 3.6 × 0.0128 = 0.415 m³.\n- Total about **0.91 m³**.\n\n- **Tools:** advanced kit, scaffold, post anchors, two or three helpers.\n- **Finish:** a temporary weather-protection coat during building is recommended for glulam [S17]; afterwards a glaze.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - no structural calculation for snow\n  - no permit check (see Q&A 17)\n  - posts without a gap above concrete or ground\n  - leaving glulam wrapped in plastic on site, which causes condensation and blue stain [S17]\n\n**7. Garden bench, backless, 120 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 45×145 for the seat and 45×95 for the frame; larch or spruce. Rounded edges are ideal.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Seat: 3 × 1.2 m of 45×145 = 3.6 m, so **1 × 4.0 m**.\n- Frame: 4 legs × 0.40 + 2 cross pieces × 0.40 + 2 stretchers × 1.0 = 4.4 m, plus 10 % = 4.8 m, so **2 × 45×95×2500**.\n- Seat height about 43–45 cm overall (rule of thumb for seating).\n\n- **Tools:** basic kit, pocket-hole jig optional.\n- **Finish:** exterior oil or glaze; end grain sealed.\n- **Mistakes:** end grain of the legs standing in wet grass (add glides or stones); seat boards without 5–8 mm gaps (rule of thumb), so water pools.\n\n**8. Adirondack chair**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed spruce or larch. Ana White's \"Ultimate DIY Adirondack chair\" needs 3 × 2x4 @ 8 ft and 1 × 1x6 @ 12 ft. The plan suggests cedar, but whitewood or spruce works \"if finished well\" [S32].\n\nArithmetic (metric conversion):\n- 3 × 2.44 m = 7.32 m of 2x4, so **2 × 45×95×4000** (8 m).\n- 1 × 3.66 m of 1x6, so **1 × 21×145×4000**.\n- Check the cut list, because 45 mm is thicker than 38 mm.\n\n- **Tools:** jigsaw (curves), drill, sander.\n- **Finish:** exterior paint (traditional) or glaze.\n- **Mistakes:** not converting the imperial cut list; sharp edges (rounded-edge stock helps).\n\n**9. Picnic table with attached benches, 180 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 45×145 and 45×95 (spruce or larch).\n\nArithmetic:\n- Top: 5 × 1.8 m. Seats: 2 × 2 × 1.8 m. That is 9 pieces of 1.8 m at 2 per 4.0 m board = **5 boards** (1 spare piece).\n- Legs (4 × about 0.85 m) and seat supports (2 × 1.55 m) = 6.5 m = **2 boards 45×145×4000**.\n- Total **7 × 45×145×4000**.\n- Cleats and braces: 3 × 0.72 + 2 × 0.8 = 3.76 m = **1 × 45×95×4000**.\n- Volume: 7 × 4 × 0.045 × 0.145 + 4 × 0.045 × 0.095 = 0.183 + 0.017 = **0.20 m³**.\n- Mass about 92 kg in spruce (0.20 × 460) or about 120 kg in larch (0.20 × 600). Plan two people to move it.\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit (mitre saw for leg angles, carriage bolts).\n- **Finish:** exterior oil or glaze; seal the end grain.\n- **Mistakes:** no gaps between boards; bolts not re-tightened after the first season (the wood shrinks); top too heavy for the leg angle.\n\n**10. Horizontal slat privacy screen, panel 1.8 × 1.8 m**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 24×70 or 28×70 slats (larch preferred); posts 90×90 or 100×100.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Pitch = 70 + 15 mm gap = 85 mm. 1800 / 85 = 21.2, so **21 slats × 1.8 m = 38 m per panel**. That is 19 × 2.0 m, or 10 × 4.0 m (2 slats per board).\n- Posts: 2 × 2.4 m per panel, sharing posts between panels; on ground anchors.\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit, string line, level.\n- **Finish:** pigmented glaze, or none for larch (it will turn grey).\n- **Mistakes:** spans too long, so slats sag (rule of thumb: posts at most about 1.8 m apart for 24 mm slats); wind load underestimated (solid screens act like sails).\n\n**11. Outdoor kitchen / BBQ counter, 200 × 60 × 90 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** frame planed 45×95; cladding 24×70 slats; **non-wood worktop** (stone or tiles) near the heat.\n\nArithmetic (cladding):\n- Faces: front 2.0 × 0.9 m + 2 sides × 0.6 × 0.9 m = 2.88 m².\n- Slats 24×70 with 10 mm gaps: 1 / 0.08 = 12.5 m/m². So 2.88 × 12.5 = **36 m**, plus 10 %, about 40 m.\n- Frame: about 18 m of 45×95 (rule-of-thumb estimate for a box frame with 3 dividers).\n\n- **Tools:** advanced kit.\n- **Finish:** exterior oil; heat shield behind the grill.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - wood too close to the grill (follow the grill maker's clearances)\n  - closed cabinets without ventilation\n  - wooden worktop next to the grill\n\n**12. Small deck / garden platform, 3 × 3 m (optional)**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** larch strongly recommended. Load-bearing decking is usually use class GK3.2 (GK4 if dirt collects) [S20], so spruce is short-lived here.\n- Typical parts (rule of thumb):\n  - Joists 45×95 or 45×145 at about 50 cm spacing on adjustable pads.\n  - Deck boards 28×145 with 7 mm gaps: 1 / 0.152 = 6.6 m/m², so 9 m² × 6.6 = **59 m**, plus 10 %, about 65 m.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - joists on soil\n  - no gaps, or gaps that fill with leaves\n  - zinc-plated screws in larch (black stains [S7])\n  - board ends butted with no gap [S19]\n\n### 3.2 Indoor furniture\n\n**13. Workbench, 160 × 60 × 90 cm, laminated softwood top**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 45×95 (spruce/fir, C24/S10 not required). Paul Sellers' popular bench laminates construction softwood for the top [S33].\n\nArithmetic:\n- Top: 600 / 45 = 13.3, so 13 pieces on edge × 1.6 m = 20.8 m.\n- Legs: doubled 45×95 → 8 × 0.80 m = 6.4 m.\n- Rails: 4 long × 1.4 m + 4 short × 0.5 m = 7.6 m.\n- Total 34.8 m, plus 10 % = 38.3 m, so **10 × 45×95×4000** (40 m).\n- Volume: 40 × 0.045 × 0.095 = **0.171 m³**, about **79 kg** of wood at 460 kg/m³. A heavy bench is a good bench.\n- Height: about 85–95 cm. Rule of thumb: roughly the user's wrist or palm height with arms hanging.\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit, at least 6–8 clamps, PVA glue, hand plane or belt sander to flatten the top (or ask for planed, square stock).\n- **Finish:** a thin oil or none.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - gluing up wet or uneven boards\n  - glue-up not clamped flat (twisted top)\n  - no racking resistance (add diagonal braces or a lower shelf)\n\n**14. Simple wall shelves, 3 shelves 80 × 20 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 28×195 (or 2 × 28×95 edge-glued).\n\nArithmetic: 3 × 0.80 = 2.4 m, so **1 × 28×195×2500**.\n\n- **Tools:** drill, level, wall anchors suited to the wall (brick, concrete, plasterboard).\n- **Finish:** oil, hard-wax oil or water-based lacquer.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - brackets too far apart (rule of thumb: 20–28 mm softwood shelves for books should not span much more than 70–80 cm between supports; check with a shelf-sag calculator)\n  - wrong wall plugs\n  - shelf cupping because a wide flat-sawn board was used (use radial stock or a glue-up)\n\n**15. Floating shelf, 80 × 22 × 4.5 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 45×195 or 45×245 (MC 8–10 %), or a glue-up from radial boards for stability.\n\nArithmetic: **1 × 0.8 m** per shelf (buy 1.0 m and trim the ends square).\n\n- **Hardware:** hidden steel rods or a rail. Drill 2 or 3 deep holes into the back edge.\n- **Tools:** drill with a guide, long bits, level, stud and pipe detector.\n- **Finish:** as for shelf 14.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - holes drilled off-angle\n  - weak wall fixing\n  - overloading: floating shelves are for decor, not encyclopaedias (rule of thumb)\n\n**16. Bookshelf, 180 × 80 × 30 cm, 5 shelves**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 28×145. Edge-glue 2 boards to get 290 mm panels. Radial boards stay flatter.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Sides: 2 × 1.8 m × 2 boards = 7.2 m.\n- Shelves: 5 × (0.80 − 2 × 0.028 = 0.744 m) × 2 boards = 7.44 m.\n- Total 14.6 m, plus 10 % = 16.1 m, so **4 × 28×145×4000**.\n- Back: 3 mm HDF panel, or 9 × 24×95 × 1.8 m slats (0.8 / 0.095 ≈ 8.4, so 9), which is 16.2 m.\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit, clamps, router (dados) or shelf-pin jig.\n- **Finish:** oil or water-based lacquer.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - not anchoring it to the wall (tip-over risk with kids)\n  - all growth rings facing the same way in a glue-up (alternate them; rule of thumb)\n  - shelves too wide or long for the load\n\n**17. Bed frame, 160 × 200 cm mattress**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 45×195 for the side and end rails; 95×95 legs (or 2 × 45×95 glued); 45×45 cleats; 24×95 slats.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Rails: 2 × 2.04 + 2 × 1.64 = 7.36 m of 45×195, so **2 × 4.0 m**.\n- Legs: 6 × 0.30 m (4 corners and 2 under the centre beam) = 1.8 m.\n- Cleats: 2 × 2.0 m of 45×45.\n- Centre beam: 1 × 2.0 m of 45×95.\n- Slats: 95 mm slat + 30 mm gap = 125 mm pitch → 2.0 / 0.125 = **16 slats × 1.6 m = 25.6 m**, so **7 × 24×95×4000** (2 slats per board, 1.6 m each).\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit, bed-rail brackets.\n- **Finish:** oil or lacquer.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - slat gaps wider than the mattress maker allows (often about 6–7 cm or less; check)\n  - no centre support on a 160 cm bed\n  - squeaks (use felt or rubber strips under the slats)\n\n**18. Low platform bed, 160 × 200 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** base frame 45×95 set back 15 cm for a \"floating\" look; deck boards 28×145.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Deck: boards along the 2.0 m length, with 2 cm ventilation gaps. 1.6 / 0.165 ≈ 10 boards × 2.0 m = **20 m**, so **5 × 28×145×4000**.\n- Frame: perimeter 2 × 1.7 + 2 × 1.3 = 6.0 m, plus 3 cross members × 1.3 m = 3.9 m; total about **10 m of 45×95**, so 3 × 4.0 m.\n\n- **Mistakes:** a closed platform under a foam mattress, which grows mould without ventilation gaps (rule of thumb); boards running the wrong way relative to the supports.\n\n**19. Kids' table, 60 × 60 × 50 cm, plus 2 stools**\n- **Difficulty:** 1–2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed spruce/fir with **rounded edges**; 45×45 legs; 28×145 tops.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Table top: 4 × 0.6 m of 28×145 = 2.4 m (edge-glued).\n- Table legs: 4 × 0.47 m = 1.88 m of 45×45.\n- Aprons: 4 × 0.5 m = 2.0 m of 24×70.\n- Stools: seats 2 × 2 × 0.3 m = 1.2 m of 28×145; legs 8 × 0.27 m = 2.16 m of 45×45.\n- To buy: **1 × 28×145×4000**, **1 × 45×45×4000**, **1 × 24×70×2000**.\n\n- **Tools:** basic kit, sander.\n- **Finish:** a finish suitable for children's toys (look for EN 71-3 on the can), or a food-safe oil.\n- **Mistakes:** sharp corners; splinters (sand to 180–240 grit); a top that overhangs so much the table tips when a child leans on it.\n\n**20. Wall coat rack / entryway bench**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 28×145 back board, 28×95 shelf, wooden pegs or hooks; or a bench as in project 7.\n\nArithmetic (rack 100 cm): 1 × 1.0 m of 28×145 and 1 × 1.0 m of 28×95, so **one 2.0 m piece of each**.\n\n- **Mistakes:** screwing only into plasterboard; hooks too close together (rule of thumb: about 15 cm).\n\n**21. Cat tree or cat wall shelves**\n- **Difficulty:** 1–2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** post 95×95 (or 2 × 45×95 glued) wrapped in sisal; base and platforms 28×145 edge-glued.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Post: 1 × 1.5 m.\n- Base 60 × 60 cm: 4 × 0.6 m = 2.4 m of 28×145.\n- 3 platforms 40 × 40 cm: 3 × 3 × 0.4 m = 3.6 m.\n- 28×145 total 6.0 m, so **2 × 3.0 m**.\n\n- **Finish:** none, or a pet-safe oil. No essential-oil products.\n- **Mistakes:** a light base so it tips over (anchor it to the wall); a slippery finish on the platforms.\n\n**22. Sauna benches (2 m long, two tiers)**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor (hot, humid).**\n- **Product:** **radial, knot-free spruce/fir, or fir** (resin-free [S6]); 28×90–95 boards on 45×70 frames.\n- Bench wood in saunas should conduct little heat and not exude resin. Retailers mostly recommend aspen, abachi or alder. Resinous woods (pine, larch) are generally avoided because resin and knots get hot [S40].\n- LWF recommends fir for sauna **lining** because it is resin-free [S6].\n- For benches, a knot-free radial fir/spruce is the best option within Solis' range (my judgement).\n\nArithmetic:\n- Upper bench 60 cm deep: boards 95 + 10 mm gap = 105 mm pitch → 0.6 / 0.105 ≈ 6 boards × 2.0 m = 12 m.\n- Lower bench 45 cm deep: about 5 boards × 2.0 m = 10 m.\n- Total **22 m of 28×95**, so 6 × 4.0 m (24 m).\n- Frames: 45×70, one frame about every 60 cm.\n\n- **Tools:** intermediate kit; round over all edges.\n- **Finish:** none, or a paraffin-based sauna oil. No lacquer.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - screws visible on the sitting surface, which burn skin (fix from below)\n  - knots and resin in the seat\n  - no gaps, so the bench does not dry\n\n**23. Picture frames, 30 × 40 cm opening, 45 mm moulding**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** radial or knot-free planed 20×45 (Solis can profile).\n\nArithmetic:\n- Outer size = 30 + 2 × 4.5 = 39 cm by 40 + 2 × 4.5 = 49 cm.\n- Mitred length = 2 × 0.39 + 2 × 0.49 = 1.76 m, plus 10 % = 1.94 m. **One 2.0 m piece per frame.**\n\n- **Tools:** mitre saw or mitre box, band clamp, router or table saw for the rebate.\n- **Finish:** any interior finish.\n- **Mistakes:** mitre angle slightly off (a 0.5° error repeats at all 8 cut faces and leaves gaps); forgetting the rebate depth for glass, picture and backing.\n\n**24. Wall cladding / decorative slat wall (interior), wall 3.0 × 2.6 m**\n- **Difficulty:** 1–2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 24×45 slats (radial gives straighter slats); 24×48 battens.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Pitch = 45 + 20 mm gap = 65 mm. 3000 / 65 = 46.2, so **47 slats × 2.6 m = 122 m**, plus 5 %, about 128 m.\n- Cross-check: 7.8 m² × 15.4 m/m² = 120 m.\n- Battens: rows every about 55 cm → 2.6 / 0.55 ≈ 5–6 rows × 3.0 m = **18 m of 24×48**.\n- Profiled tongue-and-groove alternative: use the **coverage width** (for example, a 23×95 board is about 85 mm coverage [S10]): m/m² = 1 / 0.085 = 11.8.\n\n- **Tools:** mitre saw, brad nailer or construction adhesive and screws, laser or level.\n- **Finish:** water-based lacquer or white oil. Spruce yellows with light; fir also darkens [S6].\n- **Mistakes:** not acclimatising (gaps open in winter); battens not plumb; electrics not planned.\n\n**25. Acoustic slat panel, 60 × 240 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed or radial 20×27 slats (custom dimension) on a 9–12 mm acoustic felt or PET backing.\n\nArithmetic: pitch 27 + 13 mm = 40 mm → 600 / 40 = **15 slats × 2.4 m = 36 m per panel**, so 9 × 4.0 m.\n\n- **Note:** most of the absorption comes from the porous felt or mineral wool behind the slats and the air gap. Slats alone do little (rule of thumb).\n- **Mistakes:** glue squeeze-out on the felt; uneven gaps (use a spacer jig).\n\n**26. Bunk or loft bed, kids' platform**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** planed C24 95×95 posts, 45×145 rails, 28×95 guard rails and slats.\n- **Not given as a quantity list on purpose.** Bunk beds are a safety-critical design: guard-rail heights and gaps, entrapment, ladder. Use a published plan that cites the bunk-bed safety standard (EN 747) and follow its cut list.\n- **Mistakes:** gaps that can trap a child's head; guard rails too low; wobbly frames not anchored to the wall.\n\n### 3.3 Animals, nature and garden structures\n\n**27. Dog house, medium (inside 80 × 60 cm, about 65 cm high)**\n- **Difficulty:** 2. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** frame 45×45; cladding planed 24×95 (or profiled T&G); 45×70 skids to raise the floor.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Surfaces: walls about 1.9 m², floor 0.48 m², roof 0.6 m², about 3.0 m² in total.\n- At 10.5 m/m²: 3.0 × 10.5 = 31.6 m, plus 10 % ≈ **35 m of 24×95**.\n- Frame: about 12 m of 45×45.\n\n- **Finish:** exterior paint or glaze outside, nothing inside. Roofing felt on the roof.\n- **Mistakes:** floor on the ground; door facing the prevailing wind; box too large to stay warm (rule of thumb).\n\n**28. Chicken coop for 4–6 hens, coop 1.2 × 1.0 m raised 50 cm**\n- **Difficulty:** 2–3. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed 24×95 cladding (smooth surfaces leave fewer hiding places for red mites; rule of thumb); 45×70 frame.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Walls: 2 × (1.2 × 1.2) + 2 × (1.0 × 1.2) = 5.28 m² → × 10.5 = 55 m.\n- Floor: 1.2 m² → 13 m.\n- Roof: 1.5 m² → 16 m, or a roof sheet.\n- Total about **85 m of 24×95**, plus about 20 m of 45×70.\n- Space: hobby sources often quote **about 0.3–0.4 m² of coop floor per hen and 20–25 cm of perch per hen**. Treat this as a rule of thumb and check local animal-welfare guidance.\n\n- **Finish:** outside only; removable droppings tray.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - poor ventilation\n  - chicken wire instead of welded mesh (predators)\n  - fine dusty sawdust as bedding (prefer shavings or pellets; see 4.2)\n\n**29. Beehive frames / AŽ hive parts**\n- **Difficulty:** 2 (precision). **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** knot-free radial spruce/fir laths, planed to the exact profile in your supplier's drawing. Bee space tolerances are tight, so stable radial stock helps.\n- The AŽ (Alberti-Žnideršič) standard frame is **26 × 41 cm** outside, as reported from Žnideršič's text \"Naš panj\" (verify against the current Čebelarska zveza Slovenije drawing) [S38].\n\nArithmetic:\n- One frame's perimeter = 2 × (0.41 + 0.26) = **1.34 m of lath**.\n- 10 frames = 13.4 m. A 20-frame AŽ hive = 26.8 m, plus 10 % ≈ **30 m of lath per hive**.\n\n- **Finish:** none on frames or the inside of hives. The outside of a hive can be painted (Slovenian painted hive fronts, panjske končnice, are a tradition).\n- **Mistakes:** dimensions off by 1–2 mm; resin pockets; fresh (wet) wood that shrinks out of square.\n\n**30. Nest box / birdhouse**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** planed spruce/fir board **at least 15 mm thick** (BTO); one 20×145 board, about 1.2 m.\n- Entrance holes [S34]:\n  - 25 mm for blue, coal and marsh tits\n  - 28 mm for great tit and tree sparrow\n  - 32 mm for house sparrow\n  - 45 mm for starling (larger box)\n- **No perch.** Hole at least 12 cm above the floor to protect chicks from cats [S34].\n- **Finish:** outside only, water-based; none inside.\n- **Mistakes:** a perch; a hole too low; no drainage or ventilation; no way to open it for cleaning.\n\n**31. Tool / garden shed, 2 × 2 m**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Outdoor.**\n- **Product:** KD planed or KVH 45×95 studs; 45×145 joists and rafters; 28×145 floor; 24×145 cladding with 24×48 battens (board-and-batten). Typical layout (rule of thumb):\n  - Floor joists 45×145: 5 × 2.0 m (spacing ≤ 60 cm), plus a rim of 2 × 2.0 m = 14 m.\n  - Floor boards 28×145: 2.0 / 0.145 ≈ 14 × 2.0 m = 28 m.\n  - Wall studs 45×95 at 60 cm, with door framing: about 18 × 1.9 m, plus bottom and top plates 2 × 8 m; about 50 m.\n  - Cladding: wall area 8 × 1.9 = 15.2 m², minus a 1.6 m² door = 13.6 m². Boards: × 6.9 m/m² (1 / 0.145) = 94 m of 24×145. Battens over the joints: about 82 m of 24×48.\n  - Roof: 2.4 × 2.4 = 5.8 m² of 24×145 boards = 40 m; rafters 5 × 2.5 m = 12.5 m of 45×145.\n\nArithmetic (volume):\n\n| Section | Metres | Volume |\n|---|---|---|\n| 45×145 | 26.5 m | 0.173 m³ |\n| 28×145 | 28 m | 0.114 m³ |\n| 45×95 | 50 m | 0.214 m³ |\n| 24×145 | 134 m | 0.466 m³ |\n| 24×48 | 82 m | 0.094 m³ |\n| **Total** | | **about 1.06 m³** (about 490 kg in spruce) |\n\n- **Tools:** advanced kit.\n- **Finish:** paint or pigmented glaze on the cladding; ventilated roof.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - floor framing on the ground\n  - cladding reaching the splash zone (keep 30 cm clear [S19])\n  - no permit check (Q&A 17)\n  - forgetting roof overhangs\n\n**32. Small greenhouse, 2 × 3 m, polycarbonate**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Outdoor (very humid inside).**\n- **Product:** 45×70 frame, preferably larch, or painted spruce; sill 70×70 or 45×145 larch on a plinth.\n\nArithmetic (rule-of-thumb layout):\n- Sill 10 m.\n- Posts: corners 4, plus about 1 m spacing on the long walls (2 + 2) and short walls (1 + 1) = 10 posts × 1.8 m = 18 m.\n- Top plates 10 m and mid rails 10 m.\n- Gable rafters: 4 pairs × 2 × 1.25 m = 10 m, plus a ridge of 3.0 m.\n- Total about **61 m of 45×70**, plus a 10 m sill.\n\n- **Finish:** paint, or none for larch heartwood.\n- **Mistakes:** frame spacing that does not match the polycarbonate sheet width; no roof vent; a spruce sill in contact with soil.\n- In Slovenia, a greenhouse without foundations and without utility connections is reported as a \"simple object\" under the 2022 classification (verify current rules) [S30].\n\n**33. Steep stair / loft ladder, floor-to-floor 2.6 m**\n- **Difficulty:** 3. **Indoor.**\n- **Product:** stringers of **C24/S10 planed 45×195 or 45×245, knot-free edges**; treads 45×145. Radial stock is good for the treads.\n\nArithmetic:\n- Steepness 60°: stringer length = 2.6 / sin 60° = 2.6 / 0.866 = **3.0 m**, plus about 0.1 m, so 2 × 3.1 m.\n- Rises: 2.6 / 0.22 ≈ 12 rises, so **11 treads** (the top floor is the 12th step).\n- Treads: 11 × 0.63 m (60 cm wide plus 15 mm housing each side) = 6.9 m, so **2 × 45×145×4000**.\n\n- **Tools:** advanced kit, router with a template for housings.\n- **Finish:** hard-wax oil with a slip-resistant additive.\n- **Mistakes:**\n  - uneven rises (dangerous)\n  - no handrail\n  - knots at the stringer edges\n  - not checking national building rules on stair geometry\n\n### 3.4 Small crafts and \"not ideal\" uses\n\n**34 (bonus). Cutting / serving board: softwood is not ideal.**\n- Spruce is about 1,680 N Janka versus about 6,450 N for hard maple, so knives scar it quickly. Spruce, pine and larch contain resin.\n- If a customer insists, suggest a **serving or bread board from knot-free fir**. Fir is resin-free and odour- and taste-neutral; it is traditionally used as a baking board for fish dishes [S6].\n- Finish with food-safe mineral oil. Do not use it for heavy chopping or raw meat.\n\n**35 (bonus). Pallet-style lounge from fresh sawn timber (2 m sofa)**\n- **Difficulty:** 1. **Outdoor or covered terrace.**\n- **Product:** fresh sawn 24×100 decking and 45×95 runners. HT-treated (heat only) is fine. Avoid bio-dipped wood for seating.\n\nArithmetic:\n- One \"pallet\" unit of 1.0 × 0.8 m: 3 runners × 1.0 m = 3 m of 45×95, plus 7 deck boards × 0.8 m = 5.6 m of 24×100.\n- Seat: 2 units side by side × 2 layers = 4 units, plus 1 backrest = 5 units. That is **15 m of 45×95 and 28 m of 24×100**.\n\n- **Key point:** fresh wood is wet. Let it air-dry stacked on stickers under cover for several weeks to months before building (rule of thumb; measure until under 20 %). Otherwise expect shrinkage, loosening screws, checks and possibly mould under cushions.\n- **Finish:** sand well; exterior oil once dry.\n\n---\n\n## 4. By-products for hobbyists and the home\n\n### 4.1 Pellets (A1, spruce/fir, 15 kg bags)\n\n**What A1 means** (EN ISO 17225-2 / ENplus, via certification summaries) [S23]:\n- Made from chemically untreated wood: virgin wood or untreated residues.\n- Moisture ≤ 10 %.\n- Ash ≤ 0.7 %.\n- Additives ≤ 2 % (for example starch).\n- Net calorific value ≥ 16.5 MJ/kg.\n- Diameter 6 ± 1 or 8 ± 1 mm.\n- Bulk density 600–750 kg/m³.\n- Mechanical durability ≥ 98 % (ENplus; one summary of the ISO standard says ≥ 97.5 %).\n\n**Arithmetic: energy in one bag**\n- 16.5 MJ/kg ÷ 3.6 = 4.58 kWh/kg.\n- × 15 kg = **at least about 69 kWh per bag**.\n- Rule of thumb: 1 litre of heating oil ≈ 10 kWh, so one bag ≈ 7 litres of heating oil.\n\n**Uses**\n\n| Use | Advice |\n|---|---|\n| **Heating** (pellet stove or boiler) | The intended use. Store dry; damp pellets swell and crumble. |\n| **Cat litter / small-animal litter** | Wood pellets are widely used as clumping-free litter: they swell and turn to sawdust when wet. Pet-product sellers warn that some heating pellets may contain binders, accelerants or fragrance, and that pellets made for pets are kiln-dried and dust-extracted [S37]. Advise customers to confirm with Solis whether their A1 pellets contain any additive, and to choose unscented pellets. |\n| **Horse and small-animal bedding** | Same logic as litter. Pellets need water to \"fluff\" into bedding (practice tip). |\n| **Smoker / pellet grill** | **Not recommended.** Cooking-pellet makers advise against heating pellets: they are often spruce or pine and may contain binders, and resinous softwood smoke tastes bitter [S26]. Use 100 % hardwood pellets sold for cooking. |\n| **Fire lighting / pellet baskets in log stoves** | Works in purpose-made pellet baskets. Do not overfill a log stove with pellets (practice tip). |\n\n### 4.2 Sawdust (fresh or dry)\n\n**Animal bedding**\n- Dry sawdust is absorbent and cheap.\n- Fine dust can irritate airways, so use coarser shavings or pellets for chickens and horses (practice guidance; rule of thumb).\n- Only untreated, unpainted wood.\n\n**Compost**\n- Sawdust is a strong carbon (\"brown\") material: C:N about **100–500 : 1** [S24], with other Cornell figures up to 300–700 : 1.\n- The compost target is about **30 : 1** [S24].\n- Mix thin layers with nitrogen-rich greens (grass, kitchen scraps, manure).\n- Sawdust is too fine on its own to keep a pile aerated; chips or bark give structure [S24].\n\n**Mulch on paths and around shrubs:** fine, but do not dig fresh sawdust into vegetable beds. Woody material dug into soil ties up nitrogen for months to years; as a surface mulch this is seldom a problem [S25].\n\n**Mushroom growing**\n- Oyster mushrooms grow on sawdust substrates.\n- A Romanian study found substrates partly containing softwood sawdust gave yields comparable to beech sawdust [S36].\n- Hardwood sawdust remains the usual recommendation. Spruce works best mixed with straw or hardwood and well pasteurised (cautious summary of mixed evidence).\n\n**Other uses:** absorbent for oil spills in the workshop; filler for home-made wood filler when mixed with glue. The filler should be made from the same species, so the colour matches.\n\n### 4.3 Wood chips G30 (without bark)\n\n- **G30** is the size class of the withdrawn Austrian standard ÖNORM M 7133 (1998). It is still widely quoted and has a maximum particle length of about 85 mm [S28].\n- It is roughly comparable to the EN ISO 17225-4 \"P31\" classes, but there is no exact one-to-one equivalence [S28].\n- UK smoke-control lists accept chips under 35 % moisture meeting ÖNORM M 7133 G30/G50 [S28].\n\n| Use | Advice |\n|---|---|\n| Chip boiler fuel | Use the moisture class the boiler maker specifies. |\n| Garden paths, play-area surfacing, mulch under shrubs | Debarked spruce chips are bright and clean, and break down within a few years. |\n| Compost structure | Coarse chips aerate piles of grass and kitchen waste [S24]. |\n\n### 4.4 Ground bark (mulch, fuel, fertiliser)\n\n**Mulch for shrubs, trees, berries and paths**\n- Bark C:N is about 100–130 : 1 [S24].\n- Nitrogen tie-up is mainly a problem when bark is **dug in**; as a surface layer it is seldom an issue [S25].\n- Sources disagree on whether bark mulch lowers soil pH. Some report bark pH of 2–5; a Wisconsin study found no soil pH difference [S25]. Advice: test soil pH for pH-sensitive plants, and add some nitrogen when mulching young plantings.\n- Rule of thumb: a layer of about 5–8 cm suppresses weeds. Keep it a few cm away from tree trunks and house walls.\n\n**Other uses:** fuel in suitable biomass boilers; soil-improver component in composts.\n\n### 4.5 Offcuts 7–40 cm (firewood, kindling, crafts)\n\n**Firewood**\n- Burn only below **20 % moisture** (Slovenian small-combustion regulation) [S29].\n- Spruce needs about **one year** of covered, airy stacking, preferably in the sun [S29].\n- Splitting and cutting to stove length speeds drying [S29].\n- Kiln-dried offcuts can be burned straight away.\n- Softwood burns fast and hot. It is excellent for kindling and lighting, and less ideal as the only overnight fuel (practice).\n- Spruce tends to spark: use a closed stove or a fire screen (practice).\n\n**Never burn:**\n- treated, painted or glued wood in home stoves; treated wood belongs in licensed incinerators only [S19]\n- pieces with a bio-protective (anti-blue-stain) dip (treat as chemically treated)\n\nHT-marked wood (ISPM-15) is only heat-treated: core at least **56 °C for 30 minutes** [S27]. It contains no chemical.\n\n**Crafts and kids**\n- building blocks (sand and round all edges)\n- coasters (end grain from 95×95 slices)\n- trivets\n- bird feeders\n- toy cars and boats\n- candle holders (with a metal cup insert)\n- plant labels\n- wooden spoons are better from hardwood (rule of thumb)\n\n### 4.6 Fresh sawn timber with bio-protective dip\n\n- The dip on fresh sawn timber is normally an anti-sapstain (blue-stain) treatment.\n- The bot should advise asking Solis for the product data sheet.\n- Until then, treat dipped wood like chemically treated wood:\n  - not for raised beds with vegetables\n  - not for kids' furniture\n  - not for indoor use\n  - not burned in home stoves\n- The Energieinstitut Vorarlberg advises no wood preservatives at all in living spaces [S19].\n\n---\n\n## 5. Finishing, outdoor protection and safety\n\n### 5.1 Indoor finishes (spruce, fir, pine)\n\n| Finish | Pros / cons | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hard-wax oil / oil** | Natural look, easy to repair, slight yellowing | Two thin coats; wipe off the excess. Mind oily rags (5.5). |\n| **Water-based lacquer** | Clear, low yellowing, durable on tabletops | Raises the grain: sand lightly after the first coat. |\n| **Paint** | Hides knots and colour differences | **Seal knots first with shellac or a special knot sealer.** Oil-alkyd and latex primers do not block knot extractives [S11]. For a white finish, use knot-free wood [S11]. |\n| **Stain** | Colour | Test on an offcut. Spruce and pine can blotch; use a pre-stain conditioner or gel stain [S1]. Larch stains poorly [S7]. Remove resin before staining pine and larch [S7, S8]. |\n| **Nothing** | Fine for shelves and frames | Spruce and fir darken and yellow with light [S6]. |\n\n**Resin bleed**\n- Pitch oozes when the wood gets warm, for example in sun, near radiators or in saunas, and it can come through paint [S11].\n- Kiln schedules can \"set\" pitch, but construction grades seldom have set pitch [S11].\n- Hardened pitch can be scraped off; soft pitch can be removed with turpentine or mineral spirits [S11].\n- **Shellac seals extractives but not pitch** [S11].\n- Choose fir or knot-free radial stock for warm places.\n\n### 5.2 Outdoor: constructive wood protection first\n\nAustrian, German and Swedish sources agree: **detailing beats chemicals** [S17, S19].\n\n**Rules from the Energieinstitut Vorarlberg [S19]**\n- Choose a suitable species.\n- Use heartwood outdoors (no sapwood).\n- Rough-sawn beats planed outdoors.\n- Keep a splash zone of **at least 30 cm** at the base.\n- Use large roof overhangs.\n- **Do not butt wood against wood:** capillary joints suck in water.\n- Build so wood can dry out (vapour-open).\n- **Cover end-grain faces and form drip edges.**\n- Inspect regularly.\n- Make heavily weathered parts easy to replace.\n\n**More rules**\n- Keep wood below about 20 % MC; this is the key protection against decay [S12, S20].\n- Use kiln-dried wood [S20].\n- Rules of thumb:\n  - Slope horizontal top faces (for example post tops cut at an angle or capped).\n  - Use spacers (8–10 mm) where wood meets wood or wall.\n  - Use post anchors so posts stand 2–3 cm or more clear of concrete or paving.\n  - Never set posts directly in soil or concrete.\n  - Leave ventilation gaps behind cladding.\n\n**Fasteners**\n- Use stainless steel outdoors, especially with larch: iron and tannins give blue-black stains [S7, S11].\n- If you use galvanized fasteners, use hot-dip galvanized only [S11].\n- Spruce and pine show only faint grey stains with iron [S5, S8].\n\n### 5.3 Outdoor coatings: what lasts how long\n\nUS Wood Handbook estimates (Table 16-4) for pine, fir and spruce siding, average US climate [S11]. Treat these as **relative** guidance; local exposure and climate change the numbers.\n\n| Surface | Tinted / clear penetrating finish | Semitransparent stain | Paint | Solid-colour stain |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Smooth (planed, flat grain) | 1–2 years | 2–3 years | 6–10 years | 6–8 years |\n| Saw-textured (rough) | 2–3 years | 4–7 years | 8–12 years | 8–10 years |\n\n**Notes from the same source [S11]**\n- Pigment is what protects against UV. **More pigment means a longer life**, so clear finishes last shortest.\n- The paint lives assume one primer and two top coats. A two-coat system lasts about half as long.\n- **Finish soon after installation.** In one test, paint on boards left to weather for 16 weeks before painting peeled within 3 years; on boards weathered only 1 week it peeled after 13 years; boards not pre-weathered had no peeling after 20 years. Low-density species are most sensitive to this. Prime before or right after installation.\n- **Paint wood at about its in-service moisture content.** Painting above 20 % MC raises the risk of blistering and peeling.\n- **Seal end grain.** Unsealed end grain raises the moisture of painted wood.\n- Edge-grain (radial) surfaces hold paint better than flat-grain surfaces.\n\n**Species-specific**\n- **Spruce outdoors:** opaque paints, including on rough-sawn surfaces, give good durability [S5]. Uncoated spruce turns grey or black from UV and mould [S5].\n- **Larch outdoors:** heartwood can be left untreated and will turn grey (often deliberately). Under pigmented glazes or paints, tannins can discolour; use a stain-blocking primer (practice guidance).\n- **Greying** is UV degradation of lignin plus surface mould. It is cosmetic, but erosion continues slowly [S5, S11].\n\n**What counts as wood protection**\n- Glazes, normal paints, oils and waxes are **not wood preservatives**. They are water-repellent and weather-protective coatings [S19].\n- Wood preservatives (biocides) are mostly unnecessary with good detailing. If unavoidable, use certified products and specialists [S19].\n\n### 5.4 Glulam and KVH outdoors\n\n- Glulam and KVH are made for service classes 1 and 2: indoors, or covered and sheltered [S14, S17].\n- In service class 3 (exposed), the glulam information sheet says to use durable species instead: larch or Douglas heartwood up to use class 3.1 [S17].\n- **Beginner rule:** glulam outside is fine **under a roof**, with protected beam ends and posts standing above the ground.\n- Apply a temporary weather coat during construction [S17].\n\n### 5.5 Safety basics\n\n**Wood dust**\n- UK workplace exposure limits: **5 mg/m³ for softwood dust** and **3 mg/m³ for hardwood dust**; mixtures are treated as hardwood [S21].\n- The EU binding limit for hardwood dust has been **2 mg/m³** since January 2023 [S39].\n- Health concerns include dermatitis and respiratory problems [S21]. Cleaning up also raises a lot of dust [S21].\n- **Practice:** use dust extraction or a shop vacuum on sanders and saws; wear an FFP2/FFP3 mask when sanding; vacuum instead of sweeping.\n\n**Oily rags (linseed oil, hard-wax oil) can self-ignite**\n- Soak them in water in a closed metal container, or dry them flat or hung outdoors, then dispose of them [S22].\n- Never leave them crumpled in a bin or on a pile [S22].\n\n**Machines (practice)**\n- Eye and hearing protection.\n- Riving knife and blade guard on table saws; push sticks; never stand behind the blade line (kickback).\n- Clamp the workpiece, not your hand.\n- Keep blades sharp (dull blades plus resin cause burning and kickback).\n- Unplug or remove the battery before changing blades.\n- Feed routers against the bit rotation.\n\n**Lifting:** long beams are heavy. A 100×200 glulam beam 13.5 m long weighs about 127 kg (see 2.5).\n\n**Structures:** anything people stand on or under (stairs, lofts, roofs, bunk beds) needs a proper design. Snow load on a carport roof is not a DIY guess.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Typical beginner questions (short expert answers)\n\n**1. Why did my board warp, cup or twist?**\n- It changed moisture unevenly or after you cut it.\n- Flat-sawn boards cup because tangential shrinkage is about 2× radial (spruce 0.39 vs 0.19 % per % MC) [S5].\n- Other causes:\n  - wood not dried to its service moisture\n  - one face finished and the other not\n  - stored leaning or in the sun\n  - compression wood or spiral grain [S5]\n- Fixes:\n  - buy KD at the right MC (1.6)\n  - acclimatise the wood stacked on stickers\n  - use radial boards for wide flat parts\n  - finish all faces equally\n  - alternate growth-ring orientation in glue-ups (rule of thumb)\n\n**2. Can I use spruce for a tabletop?**\n- Yes, it is common and cheap, but it is **soft** (Janka 1,680 N vs about 6,450 N for maple) and will dent.\n- Use a hard finish (water-based lacquer or hard-wax oil). Use radial or edge-glued boards at 8–10 % MC.\n- Fix the top so it can move: an 800 mm wide top can move about 9–12 mm seasonally (1.5).\n- For a harder softwood, choose larch (3,290 N) or pine (2,420 N).\n\n**3. How do I calculate how much wood I need?**\n1. Draw the piece.\n2. List every part (count × section × length).\n3. Group the parts by section.\n4. Nest the lengths into the stock lengths you can buy (2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0 m).\n5. Add 10–15 % waste.\n\nFor cladding or decking, m per m² = 1 / (board width + gap) in m. See the worked examples in section 3.\n\n**4. Do I need a planer?**\n- Only if you buy rough-sawn timber and want furniture-quality flat, square, smooth parts.\n- For outdoor structures, rough-sawn is fine and holds stains better [S11, S19].\n- Otherwise order **planed** timber, cut to length if possible.\n\n**5. How long should wood acclimatise?**\n- Rule of thumb: 1–2 weeks in the room where it will be used, stacked with air gaps; longer for thick or wetter wood.\n- Better: measure with a moisture meter until it reads about the expected service MC:\n  - about 8–10 % in heated rooms\n  - about 12–16 % for covered outdoor use [S10, S11, S13]\n\n**6. Can glulam go outside?**\n- Yes, under a roof: it is designed for service classes 1–2 [S14, S17].\n- Fully exposed spruce glulam is not recommended. For exposed use, choose durable species (larch or Douglas heartwood) [S17] or protect the beams constructively (cover boards, end caps) and coat them.\n\n**7. Spruce or larch for my raised bed?**\n- Larch heartwood lasts longer.\n- Spruce in soil contact is a consumable: heartwood stakes in soil last under 5 years [S9], and hobby sources quote 3–5 years for untreated pine or fir beds [S31].\n- A dimpled liner on the inside helps.\n- Thicker boards (28 mm or more) last longer and bow less.\n\n**8. Which moisture content should I order?**\n\n| Use | MC |\n|---|---|\n| Indoor furniture | 8–10 % |\n| Interior cladding and structure | 10–12 % |\n| Covered outdoor and garden furniture | 12–16 % |\n| Rough outdoor | 16–20 % |\n\nSee 1.6 for the sources behind these values.\n\n**9. What's the difference between S10 and C24?**\n- S10 is the visual grade under DIN 4074-1. C24 is the European strength class it maps to (for spruce/fir) via EN 1912 [S16].\n- C24 means a characteristic bending strength of 24 N/mm² [S16].\n- You need it for load-bearing parts. For furniture it is irrelevant.\n\n**10. Why is my \"45×95\" not 50×100?**\n- Planed sizes are net after planing.\n- Sawn nominal sizes refer to a moisture of about 20 % [S43], and the wood shrinks further as it dries (1.5).\n\n**11. Can I build furniture from fresh sawn wood?**\n- Not indoor furniture: it will shrink by several percent across the grain and crack.\n- Air-dry it first (stickered, under cover; months), or use it only for rough outdoor projects such as a compost bin or pallet lounge.\n\n**12. Treated or untreated wood for vegetable beds?**\n- Untreated larch or untreated spruce (as a consumable) is the low-chemical option.\n- If wood is pressure-treated or dipped, line the soil side [S31] and ask for the product data sheet.\n\n**13. What finish for outdoor furniture?**\n- Pigmented exterior oil or glaze, renewed every 1–3 years (rule of thumb, consistent with [S11]).\n- Or paint for the longest life (6–12 years on softwood in US data) [S11].\n- Seal all end grain. Clear finishes last shortest [S11].\n- Larch can be left untreated and allowed to grey [S7].\n\n**14. My white paint turned yellow or brown over the knots. Why?**\n- Knot extractives migrate through oil and latex primers [S11].\n- Seal the knots with shellac or a special knot sealer, then prime and apply two top coats [S11].\n- Or use knot-free wood for white finishes.\n\n**15. Resin is oozing from my spruce or pine. What now?**\n- Heat drives it out [S11].\n- Let it harden and scrape it off; remove soft resin with turpentine or mineral spirits; then sand, spot-prime and recoat [S11].\n- It can recur in summer.\n- For hot places (sauna, south-facing, near radiators) choose fir, which has no resin canals [S6], or knot-free radial stock.\n\n**16. Which screws outdoors?**\n- Stainless steel (A2, or A4 near pools and the coast).\n- Mandatory for larch, to avoid blue-black iron-tannin stains [S7].\n- Pre-drill larch near ends and for large screws [S7].\n- If galvanized, use hot-dip galvanized only [S11].\n\n**17. Do I need a permit for a carport, shed or greenhouse (Slovenia)?**\n- The 2022 Decree on the classification of buildings (Uredba o razvrščanju objektov) introduced \"simple objects\" (enostavni objekti) that are built without a building permit but must still comply with spatial plans [S30].\n- Reported conditions:\n  - Simple objects are single-storey, up to about 4 m high.\n  - Greenhouses without foundations or utility connections are simple objects.\n  - Canopies (nadstrešnica): about 20 m² is cited as the simple limit, with larger sizes non-demanding and needing a permit. Sources differ.\n- **Always check with the municipality or the upravna enota before building** [S30].\n\n**18. Is radial (quarter-sawn) wood worth the extra cost?**\n- For wide or visible flat parts (tabletops, doors, frames, cladding, sauna benches, beehive frames): yes. It moves about half as much across its width as flat-sawn wood and hardly cups [S5, S11].\n- For frames and garden structures: no.\n\n**19. Can I burn offcuts, or use pellets in my smoker?**\n- Offcuts: yes, if untreated and under 20 % MC [S29]. Never burn painted, glued, treated or dipped wood [S19].\n- Heating pellets in a smoker: no. Use food-grade hardwood pellets; softwood resin gives a bitter taste [S26].\n\n**20. Can I glue spruce and larch?**\n- Yes.\n- Spruce glues easily with PVA and thermoset glues [S5]. Larch glues without general problems [S7].\n- Very resinous pine may glue poorly [S8].\n- Glue fresh-planed surfaces, at matching moisture, within a day of planing (rule of thumb).\n\n**21. How do I stop end grain splitting?**\n- Pre-drill near ends, especially in larch [S7].\n- Keep screws at least about 5× their diameter from the end (rule of thumb).\n- Seal end grain outdoors (paint or end-grain sealer) and protect it from standing water [S11, S19].\n- Choose boxed-heart-free (herzfrei) pieces for posts to reduce checking [S14].\n\n**22. My pine has blue or grey streaks. Is it rotten?**\n- No. It is blue stain: a fungus that does not reduce strength and is allowed without limit in structural grades [S8].\n- It shows the wood was wet once. Dry it and finish with a pigmented coating if the look bothers you.\n\n**23. Are knots a strength problem?**\n- For furniture, rarely. Loose or black knots may fall out; fill them or glue them in.\n- For beams and stair stringers, large knots at the edges significantly lower strength. That is why grading (S10/C24) exists.\n- Knot-free radial stock is the premium option for stairs and sauna benches.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n**Species properties**\n- [S1] The Wood Database: Norway spruce (*Picea abies*). https://www.wood-database.com/norway-spruce/\n- [S2] The Wood Database: European silver fir (*Abies alba*). https://www.wood-database.com/european-silver-fir/\n- [S3] The Wood Database: Scots pine (*Pinus sylvestris*). https://www.wood-database.com/scots-pine/\n- [S4] The Wood Database: European larch (*Larix decidua*). https://www.wood-database.com/european-larch/\n- [S5] Richter, K. et al.: Das Holz der Fichte – Eigenschaften und Verwendung. LWF Wissen 80, Bayerische Landesanstalt für Wald und Forstwirtschaft / Holzforschung München. Values after DIN 68364 and Grosser & Zimmer 1998. https://lfl.bayern.de/mam/cms04/forsttechnik-holz/dateien/w80_richter_holz_der_fichte.pdf\n- [S6] Grosser, D.: Das Holz der Tanne – Eigenschaften und Verwendung. LWF. https://lfl.bayern.de/mam/cms04/forsttechnik-holz/dateien/w45_das_holz_der_tanne_eigenschaften_verwendung.pdf\n- [S7] Das Holz der Lärche – Eigenschaften und Verwendung. LWF Wissen 69. https://lfl.bayern.de/mam/cms04/waldschutz/dateien/das_holz_der_laerche_w69.pdf\n- [S8] Das Holz der Kiefer – Eigenschaften und Verwendung. LWF Wissen 57. https://lwf.bayern.de/mam/cms04/waldschutz/dateien/lwf-wissen_57-12.pdf\n\n**Durability and moisture**\n- [S9] Swedish Wood: Durability and resistance (EN 350 table, ground-contact lifetimes). https://www.swedishwood.com/wood-facts/about-wood/from-log-to-plank/durability-and-resistance/\n- [S10] Swedish Wood: Moisture content (indoor MC through the year, delivery MC targets, measuring). https://www.swedishwood.com/wood-facts/about-wood/wood-and-moisture/\n- [S11] USDA Forest Products Laboratory: Wood Handbook – Wood as an Engineering Material, FPL-GTR-190 (2010). Ch. 13: recommended MC (Table 13-2) and dimensional-change coefficients (Table 13-5). Ch. 16: finishing, service life of finishes (Table 16-4), pre-weathering, knots and pitch, fasteners. The FPL server refused automated download, so the full-text copy used was https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/sites/default/files/barcu/regact/2014/capandtradeprf14/usfswoodhandbook2010.pdf (the 2021 edition, FPL-GTR-282, is on fpl.fs.usda.gov).\n- [S12] Baunetz Wissen Holz: Feuchtetechnische Eigenschaften von Holz (FSP about 30 %, install at service MC, ≤ 20 % rule). https://www.baunetzwissen.de/holz/fachwissen/werkstoff-holz/feuchtetechnische-eigenschaften-von-holz-8620963\n- [S13] Baunetz Wissen: Trockenes Holz (interior 8–12 %, exterior 12–15 %). Seen via search snippet. https://www.baunetzwissen.de/glossar/t/trockenes-holz-6989726\n\n**Engineered and graded timber**\n- [S14] Überwachungsgemeinschaft Konstruktionsvollholz / Holzbau Deutschland: holzbau handbuch R4 T2 F1 \"Konstruktionsvollholz KVH und Balkenschichtholz (Duobalken, Triobalken)\". Covers MC 15 ± 3 %, pith rules, preferred sections, Duo/Trio limits and service classes. PDF copy: https://www.fries24.de/out/media/r04_t02_f01_konstruktionsvollholz_kvh_und_balkenschichtholz_-_duobalken_-_triobalken_-.pdf\n- [S15] KVH: Was ist KVH (definition, service classes). https://www.kvh.eu/kvh/\n- [S16] JAF / Frischeis: Festigkeitsklassen nach EN 338, Sortierung lt. ÖNORM DIN 4074 (C16/S7, C24/S10, C30/S13 values). https://www.frischeis.at/pim/10%20Holzbau/datenblaetter/Holzbau/Festigkeitsklassen%20Schnittholz.pdf\n- [S17] Studiengemeinschaft Holzleimbau: Glulam information sheet (English). Covers surface qualities, crack limits, weather protection, service classes, use of durable species. https://www.mm-holz.com/fileadmin/Bilder/Service/Technische_Dokumente/BSH-Merkblatt_en_stghb.pdf\n- [S18] Structuralbasics: Glulam strength capacities according to Eurocode (EN 14080 GL24h values). https://www.structuralbasics.com/?p=6663\n\n**Protection, use classes and safety**\n- [S19] Energieinstitut Vorarlberg: Wegweiser Ökologisch Bauen – Holzschutz (2019). Constructive protection rules, 30 cm splash zone, treated wood not to be burnt. https://www.energieinstitut.at/media/wegweiser-oekologisch-bauen-holzschutz.pdf/@@download/file/Wegweiser-Ökologisch-bauen-Holzschutz.pdf\n- [S20] Baunetz Wissen Holz: Gebrauchsklassen und Anwendungsbereiche; Holzbauteile GK 0 im Außenbereich (DIN 68800). https://www.baunetzwissen.de/holz/fachwissen/holz-im-aussenbereich/holzbauteile-gebrauchsklasse-gk-0-im-aussenbereich-8605295 and https://www.baunetzwissen.de/drucken/gebrauchsklassen-und-anwendungsbereiche-8603832\n- [S21] UK HSE: COSHH and woodworkers – key messages (WELs 5 mg/m³ softwood, 3 mg/m³ hardwood). https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/industry/woodworking.htm\n- [S22] Fire services on oily rags: Northamptonshire Fire & Rescue https://www.northantsfire.gov.uk/2020/06/23/warning-to-home-improvers-about-oil-cloth-combustion-risks ; Bend Fire Department https://bendoregon.gov/home/showdocument?id=16299\n- [S39] CIOP-PIB (Poland): note on Directive (EU) 2017/2398, binding hardwood-dust limit 2 mg/m³ from 2023. https://chemia.ciop.pl/CIOPPortalWAR/file/92353/2021070912220&PIMOS_2_2021_27_138.pdf\n\n**By-products and fuels**\n- [S23] Pellet A1 requirements:\n  - DIN CERTCO certification scheme, wood pellets Annex 1: https://www.dincertco.de/media/dincertco/dokumente_1/certification_schemes/2-pfg-c-0444_annex-1_en_wood-pellets.pdf\n  - ENplus test report example: https://warmeston.ee/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/BEA2024044-2-test-report.pdf\n  - EN ISO 17225-2 summary: https://encyclopedia.pub/entry/history/show/105371\n- [S24] Cornell Waste Management Institute: Composting – chemistry (C:N table) and Composting ingredients. https://www.css.cornell.edu/cwmi/compost/chemistry.html and https://www.compost.css.cornell.edu/Factsheets/FS2.html\n- [S25] University of Idaho Extension: mulch bulletin (nitrogen tie-up when incorporated vs surface mulch). https://objects.lib.uidaho.edu/uiext/uiext30220.pdf ; UW Extension newsletter (no measured N deficiency or pH change from mulches). https://shawano.extension.wisc.edu/files/2010/11/July-August-2010.pdf\n- [S26] Camp Chef: Guide to smoker pellets (heating vs cooking pellets). https://www.campchef.com/blog/bbq-wood-pellets-guide/\n- [S27] IPPC: ISPM 15 Annex 1, heat treatment of 56 °C for 30 min. https://www.ippc.int/static/media/files/publication/en/2016/10/_DraftISPM_ISPM15Annex1_.pdf ; USDA Forest Service efficacy note: https://www.fs.usda.gov/nrs/highlights/2240\n- [S28] Woodsure: ISO/EN 17225 factsheet (ÖNORM M 7133 G30 limits). https://www.woodsure.co.uk/app/uploads/factSheetISOEN17225.pdf ; DEFRA smoke-control permitted fuels (G30/G50 chips under 35 % MC). https://smokecontrol.defra.gov.uk/permitted_fuels/wood-chip-less-than-35-moisture-content-and-meeting-onorm-m-7133-1998-size-class-g30-wood-pellets-of-6-mm-diameter-and-a-moisture-content-below-10-72\n- [S29] RTV SLO: Kako s pravilno uporabo kurilnih naprav zmanjšati onesnaženost zraka (spruce firewood drying about 1 year). https://www.rtvslo.si/okolje/kako-s-pravilno-uporabo-kurilnih-naprav-zmanjsati-onesnazenost-zraka/758592 ; dLib record on firewood moisture under 20 % per the Slovenian small-combustion decree. https://www.dlib.si/stream/URN:NBN:SI:DOC-3M4ELAWU/5a997823-7690-4471-ae25-b34a7724034d/PDF\n- [S36] USAMV Bucharest: oyster mushroom yields on substrates including softwood sawdust (2015). https://biotechnologyjournal.usamv.ro/pdf/2015/Art61.pdf\n- [S37] Small Pet Select (commercial): pine pellets for litter vs wood-stove pellets. https://help.smallpetselect.com/en-US/small-pet-select-pine-pellets-for-litter-boxes-why-they-are-safer-than-wood-stove-pellets-or-other-companies-pine-pellets-386233\n\n**Regulations and project references**\n- [S30] Žurnal24: Pod temi pogoji lahko postavimo nadstrešek, vrtno uto, rastlinjak, garažo (27 March 2022, on the new Uredba o razvrščanju objektov). https://www.zurnal24.si/pod-streho/gradimo-obnavljamo/pod-temi-pogoji-lahko-postavimo-nadstresek-vrtno-uto-rastlinjak-garazo-383616 ; Tax-Fin-Lex: enostavni objekt. https://www.tax-fin-lex.si/pravni-termini/enostavni-objekt-92bda836\n- [S31] RHS: How to make a raised bed. https://www.rhs.org.uk/garden-features/raised-beds ; Kansas City Community Gardens build guide (lifespans, board thickness). https://kccg.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/Raised-Bed_How-to-Build_FINAL.pdf ; Garden Gate. https://www.gardengatemagazine.com/articles/projects/all/how-to-build-a-wood-raised-garden-bed\n- [S32] Ana White: Ultimate DIY Adirondack chair plans (shopping list). https://www.ana-white.com/woodworking-projects/ultimate-diy-adirondack-chair-plans\n- [S33] Paul Sellers: How to build a workbench (laminated softwood top). https://paulsellers.com/2012/06/making-your-workbench/\n- [S34] BTO: Britain needs more holes (nest-box hole sizes, 15 mm timber, no perch). https://bto.org/about-bto/press-releases/britain-needs-more-holes ; Natural History Museum: how to make a nesting box. https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/how-to-make-a-bird-nesting-box.html\n- [S35] Beginner project roundups (popularity check): Kreg Tool https://learn.kregtool.com/?p=27109 ; Baird Brothers https://blog.bairdbrothers.com/popular-woodworking-projects-to-try-this-weekend/\n- [S38] Žnideršič, A.: \"Naš panj\" (AŽ hive description, frame 26 × 41 cm), via dLib.si search results. Exact document link not verified; confirm frame drawings with Čebelarska zveza Slovenije (https://czs.si).\n- [S40] Sauna wood guides (commercial, consistent with each other): Osmo sauna panelling https://www.osmo.de/en/wood/interior-wood/walls-ceilings/sauna-panelling ; Thermory https://thermory.com/blog/interior/types-of-wood-for-saunas/ ; Sauna Marketplace https://saunamarketplace.com/sauna-wood-types/\n- [S43] Puuinfo (Finland): Standard sizes – thicknesses, widths and lengths (nominal at 20 % MC; lengths 2.7–5.4 m in 300 mm steps). https://puuinfo.fi/puutieto/sawn-timber/standard-sizes-thicknesses-widths-and-lengths/?lang=en\n\n**Reliability notes**\n- Commercial sources ([S26], [S37], [S40]) are used only where they agree with each other and with the physics. They are labelled as commercial.\n- The Slovenian permit summary [S30] is a press article, not the legal text.\n- Rules of thumb are marked in the text. A chatbot should present them as approximate and suggest measuring (moisture meter) or asking a professional (structures, permits).\n";
